    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Security-Officers--St-Lucia-_R-61365-1": {
      "title": "Casual Security Officers (St Lucia)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-17\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nProperty and Facilities Casual rate of $46.47 per hour plus 12% Super Multiple Casual opportunities based at St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity As a Casual Security Officer, you will provide professional and responsive security services across UQ's St Lucia Campus. The St Lucia security team supports a large-scale 24/7 operation, with officers responding to incidents, conducting patrols, securing buildings, assisting staff, students and visitors, monitoring security systems, and supporting emergency responses through the University's Emergency Operations Centre. You will play an important role in maintaining a safe and welcoming campus environment while delivering high quality customer service and exercising sound judgement in a dynamic operational setting. Key responsibilities include: Responding to alarms, medical emergencies, fire emergencies and other incidents. Conducting campus patrols and securing University buildings and grounds. Providing access to authorised personnel. Monitoring security systems and supporting operations within the Emergency Operations Centre. Responding professionally to enquiries and concerns from staff, students and visitors. Escalating urgent or high-priority matters to the Security Supervisor. Supporting the enforcement of parking and traffic regulations. Conducting preliminary investigations as directed. Preparing clear, detailed and accurate incident reports. Performing other reasonable duties as directed by the security leadership team. About You You will be a calm, reliable and professional security officer who can confidently make decisions in a busy operational environment and provide exceptional service to a diverse university community. To be eligible, you must hold: A current Queensland Certificate II in Security Operations, including Security Officer Unarmed, Crowd Control, Bodyguard and Monitoring units. HLTAID011 Provide First Aid. HLTAID009 Provide Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR). A current Queensland manual open driver licence. You will also demonstrate: Well-developed knowledge of security methodologies and practices. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Confidence using security monitoring systems and related technologies. The ability to prepare accurate reports and maintain appropriate records. Sound judgement and effective decision-making skills. The ability to work independently and as part of a 24/7 operational team. Professionalism, tact and restraint when managing challenging situations. Experience responding to emergency or operational incidents. A genuine commitment to providing a safe and welcoming campus environment. Important Training Requirement Successful applicants must be available to attend an initial five-week period of full-time training. Following completion of training, any work offered will be casual and based on operational requirements. The training requirement does not mean that ongoing full-time hours will be available. The successful candidate will be required to complete background checks including verification of their right to work in Australia and a criminal history check. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you'll have the opportunity to work alongside some of the brightest minds from around the world, contributing to a culture of excellence, collaboration and innovation. Working at UQ means joining an organisation committed to creating a better future through education, research and community impact. Employees also enjoy access to a range of benefits and rewards, including exclusive internal career opportunities and affordable parking options. Questions? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Mr Andrew Barling at a.barling@pf.uq.edu.au .  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number in the subject line. Want to Apply? All applicants must upload the following document for their application to be considered: Resume Other Information UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges that have impacted, or continue to impact, their career trajectory. Candidates who do not meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunity when assessing suitability for the role. We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion, fostering an environment that reflects our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining and promoting diverse talent. Reach out to talent@uq.edu.au  for accessibility support or adjustments. #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Research-Phlebotomists---Tackle-Concussion-Trial_R-65434-1": {
      "title": "Casual Research Phlebotomists – Tackle Concussion Trial",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nQueensland Brian Institute Multiple casual positions available at HEW Level 4 The hourly casual rate will range from $51.24 - $54.33 plus 12% Superannuation Administratively based at our St Lucia campus however travel will be required to schools and study sites across Brisbane About This Opportunity    We are seeking motivated and reliable Research Phlebotomists , with previous experience in the collection of bio-samples (blood, saliva, finger-prick), to support the UQ Concussion Study at our world-leading Queensland Brian Institute on a casual basis. The UQ Concussion Study is based on a partnership between World Rugby, Rugby Australia, the Great Public Schools Association (GPS) and focuses on developing a biomarker for concussion diagnosis and its recovery. The study will recruit participants from schools in South-East Queensland, and a multitude of data elements such as demographics, cognitive data, and bio-specimen samples, will be collected from participants at different timepoints. In this role, you will provide support to school staff and participants at participating schools. Key duties will include bio-sample collection including phlebotomy, record keeping, facilitating data collection processes at schools and providing support to the study sites when needed. These are casual positions and as such, work hours will vary from week to week depending on participant availability. Please note, due to the nature of the study, applicants will need to be able to work flexible out of hours and/or on Saturdays during the GPS rugby season (May to September). Applicants must also have access to their own reliable transport to travel to study sites. About You  Ideal candidates will be reliable, organised and able to work self-directed in a busy environment. You must be able to work with school staff and adolescent participants in a respectful and supportive manner, and you will be able to provide clear and easy-to-follow instructions regarding all study procedures to both school staff and participants/parents as required. In addition, you will possess: Certificate III in Pathology Collection (HLT37215) or an equivalent Australian recognised qualification. Previous training and experience in phlebotomy is essential. Conducting venepuncture on adolescents would be highly regarded. Training in all other study procedures will be provided. Excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to work autonomously i.e. flexibility to attend study sites at short notice, work autonomously and in small teams with a wide range of varied stakeholders. Strong organisational skills i.e. ability to keep organised records, and report self-directed, in timely manner. Proficient in the use of the Microsoft office suite products including Word, Excel and Outlook. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Prof Fatima Nasrallah at f.nasrallah@uq.edu.au or Dr Alex Kanon at a.kanon@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number ( R-65434 ) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Other Information As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work, please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  Pre‑employment checks will include verification of the right to work in Australia (employer-sponsored work rights are not available for these appointments), mandatory immunisations and a Working with Children Blue Card. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.  We are dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ. We encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Application reference number: R-65434 Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early.   #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Casual-Associate-Animal-Technician_R-60694": {
      "title": "Casual Associate Animal Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-02-02\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nQueensland Animal Science Precinct (QASP) Join our casual pool for a range of associate animal technician opportunities   Competitive hourly rate of $44.67 , plus 11.5% superannuation Based at our Gatton Campus   About This Opportunity    We are seeking reliable and committed Associate Animal Technicians to join our casual pool, providing essential care and husbandry for research animals at the Queensland Animal Science Precinct (QASP). This role ensures the welfare of animals while supporting general facility operations, including the use of equipment, chemicals, and other materials. You'll contribute to maintenance tasks and the daily functionality of the QASP, working under routine or general supervision.    These casual roles are located at the Gatton Campus and may require some work outside of normal working hours, including participation on the weekend/public holiday roster.    Duties and Responsibilities Include:  Animal Husbandry   Provide care and welfare for livestock, ensuring clean housing and maintaining biosecurity.  Perform record-keeping and assist with research sampling and euthanasia as needed.  Operational Support   Prepare facilities, operate machinery, and ensure compliance with safety and regulatory standards.  Support daily operations, including waste management and effective team communication.  Research Support   Assist in research projects by performing routine procedures and providing experimental support.  Participate in rosters, including weekend/public holiday duties, as required.  About UQ's Gatton Campus Established over 125 years ago, UQ’s Gatton Campus has a long history – from starting out as a dedicated agricultural institution, to being a field hospital during WWII, and to joining the UQ community in 1990.     Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our casual staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you’ll have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.      Join an organisation where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and intercampus bus    On campus childcare , medical clinic, and post office   Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sports facility    Volunteer in our community garden    About You    Essential:   Certificate III in Agriculture (or equivalent) and relevant work experience.  Possession of a C Class Licence and at least 2 years’ experience in livestock handling and animal husbandry.  Competence in livestock techniques (e.g., SC/IM injections, blood collection).  Experience operating plant and machinery (e.g., tractors, forklifts) and capacity to obtain required licences/permits.  Ability to safely operate and use hand tools and basic equipment required for animal husbandry, facility maintenance support, and routine technical tasks, in accordance with OH&S standards. Strong attention to detail, ability to work independently, and excellent team collaboration and communication skills.  Desirable:   Knowledge of OH&S, OGTR, DAWE regulatory requirements, and animal welfare codes.  Experience in a research environment or meat processing. The successful candidate may be required to complete a number of pre-employment checks, including: right to work in Australia and Medical Clearance.  You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     Medical Clearance: Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the Employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position.    Vaccinations and Immunisation: It is a condition of employment for this role that if you are required now or in the future, to work or interact in Queensland Health clinical facility; or in an equivalent, clinical health facility; or health care role; or will be required to perform work tasks that put you at risk of exposure to vaccine-preventable disease you are required to be immunised against, and remain immunised against, certain vaccine-preventable diseases (VPDs) in accordance with the University’s Vaccinations and Immunisation Guidelines (PPL 2.60.08). The employee is required to provide evidence of immunisation against VPDs.  Questions?    For more information about this opportunity, please contact talent@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     Want to Apply?    All applicants must upload the following documents in order for your application to be considered:  Cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section   Resume   Other Information    UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.   We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. Reach out to talent@uq.edu.au for accessibility support or adjustments.  Applications for this casual pool will be reviewed on an ongoing basis (R-60694). #LI-DNI"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Dental-Assistants_R-52986": {
      "title": "Casual Dental Assistants",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-01-21\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nSchool of Dentistry Casual position with an hourly rate in the range of $43.79 to $48.20, plus superannuation of 11.5% (HEW Level 3) Based at the Oral Health Centre, Herston campus About This Opportunity The University of Queensland's School of Dentistry is inviting expressions of interest from qualified dental assistants who are interested in casual opportunities. As a Casual Dental Assistant, you will support students and UQ clinical staff at the School of Dentistry’s student-led Dental Clinic. Your responsibilities will include (but are not limited to): Supporting clinical operations to ensure a smooth, effective teaching and treatment environment. Set up, clean, sterilise, and store dental instruments and equipment in line with infection control standards. Maintain accurate clinic records and uphold professional presentation standards. Book, receive and prepare patients for treatment under the supervision of the Clinical Coordinator. About You We welcome applications from Dental Assistants who hold a Certificate III in Dental Assisting (or equivalent Dental Assisting Qualification) and have hands-on experience as a chairside Dental Assistant. A Certificate IV in Dental Assisting (Radiography stream) would be desirable. Experience with Chairside Assisting Minimum of 1–2 years’ experience assisting during general and/or specialty dental procedures, with strong skills in instrument handling, sterilisation, and patient preparation Excellent Interpersonal and Communication Skills Demonstrated ability to communicate clearly and compassionately with patients and team members Willingness to Learn and Grow Professionally Demonstrates a proactive attitude toward learning new skills, techniques, and technologies in a dynamic clinical environment. Open to ongoing training and development opportunities. The successful candidate may be required to complete background checks prior to commencement including the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations, and Blue Card check. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world. Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our professional staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you’ll have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.  Questions? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Anna Barrett at a.barrett@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    Want to Apply? We welcome applications from all individuals and are committed to an inclusive and accessible recruitment process. To be considered, please ensure you upload: Resume  Our strength as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. If you require an alternative method to submit your application due to accessibility needs or personal circumstances, please contact talent@uq.edu.au . Other Information   As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.  UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role. Application reference number R-52986. Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Dentistry_R-59295": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Dentistry",
      "description": "Start date: 2025-12-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity  The University of Queensland School of Dentistry is seeking expressions of interest from casual academics to be engaged on a casual basis. Casual Academics at the School of Dentistry play a significant part in the overall student experience and assist in the teaching and delivery of both undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):   Preparing and delivering guest lecturers, workshops, seminars and tutorials. Undertaking marking of assessment tasks, as directed by course coordinators, with hours for marking to be confirmed by School Administration staff prior to commencing marking. Casual academic positions are based at both the Herston and St Lucia Campuses and successful appointees may be required to attend on campus. Our Ideal Candidate  The School welcomes applications from candidates who possess appropriate qualifications and experience within the dental industry. High-achieving postgraduate coursework and research higher degree students are also invited to apply. What We Can Offer  These are casual positions. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate can be viewed here . Compliance  All Casual Academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:  The University’s Code of Conduct .  Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.  The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .  Requirements of the Education Services  for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.  About the School of Dentistry The UQ School of Dentistry has a long and proud tradition of over 80 years of strength in dental education and research and attracts high achieving students nationally and internationally. UQ is a leading source of expertise in the local, national, and international dental community and has been a key contributor to achieving effective solutions to contemporary issues and challenges. The School has strong collaborations within and outside the dental profession. As part of the Faculty of Health, Medicine and Behavioural Sciences, the School of Dentistry offers the Bachelor of Dental Science (Honours) program and specialist post-graduate programs Doctor of Clinical Dentistry and the Doctor of Dental Medicine. The School of Dentistry also has a growing cohort of research higher degree students. Further details about the School’s teaching programs, research interests and other activities may be accessed on the School’s website at http://www.dentistry.uq.edu.au . Questions?  If you have any questions regarding this expression of interest, please contact the School of Dentistry, Acting Student and Academic Administration Manager, Mrs Anna Jackson at anna.jackson@uq.edu.au . Want To Apply?     All applicants must provide a resume to apply.  The School of Dentistry employs Casual Academics as required throughout the year. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Heron-Island-Research-Station/Research-Station-Support-Services-Assistant--Heron-Island----Identified--s25-_R-62194-3": {
      "title": "Research Station Support Services Assistant (Heron Island) - Identified (s25)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Science 2 x Full time permanent positions Base salary will be in the range $79,096.42- $83,863.22 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 4)  This position is open to Traditional Owners of Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng or Taribelang Bunda Peoples – the Traditional Custodians of the land and sea Country encompassing Heron Island and surrounding areas. Based at Heron Island Research Station Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity    Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team at UQ’s Heron Island Research Station, where scientific excellence meets cultural stewardship on Country. This identified role supports the day-to-day operations of UQ’s research station while embedding Traditional Owner knowledge, values, and perspectives into environmental management and research activities. We are seeking two positions with complementary focus areas: One position with a stronger emphasis on maintenance and facility operations . One position with a stronger emphasis on administration, outreach, and research support services . Working as part of a collaborative team, both Research Station Support Services Assistant roles contribute to the overall daily operations of UQ’s research stations. This work may be supporting the delivery of specific services including scientific (e.g. laboratory and aquaria), boating, snorkelling and diving, administrative, general maintenance and other operational tasks and activities required to maintain the safe and effective functioning of the research station. Key responsibilities include: Assisting with operational services including administration, logistics, and facility upkeep across the station Supporting the preparation, use, and maintenance of scientific, boating, and diving equipment to enable research and teaching activities Contributing to culturally informed environmental management practices and promoting awareness of cultural heritage protocols Collaborating with staff, researchers, and visitors to ensure safe, efficient, and respectful station operations On-site accommodation will be provided by UQ while undertaking work on Heron Island . Take our virtual tour . For further information, please  click here  to view the information booklet.  About You    The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the  Anti-Discrimination Act 1991  (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.  Must be a Traditional Owner of the Bailai, Gurang, Gooreng Gooreng, or Taribelang Bunda Peoples – the Traditional Custodians of the land and sea Country encompassing Heron Island and surrounding areas. Connection to and knowledge of the cultural heritage, traditions, and values of the Traditional Owners of Heron Island and surrounding areas. Vocational training qualification (or ready ability to acquire) in science, business, or a trade with relevant experience, OR an equivalent combination of relevant experience, education, and training. Demonstrated ability to follow complex processes and systems, ideally where necessary for health and safety, animal ethics, and/or environmental compliance. Current holder of (or ready ability to attain and maintain) the necessary qualifications and experience for formal recognition in the operation of plant and heavy machinery. High level computer literacy skills including the ability to use, or learn to use, software applications associated with analytical instruments and business operations. Demonstrated high level of interpersonal skills, with a proven ability to develop and sustain strategic relationships, to work collaboratively and undertake effective consultations with client groups. About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?  For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr  Caitlin Lawson at  caitlin.lawson@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.     Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 21 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-62194).   Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late September/early October."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Customs-House/Casual-Food-and-Beverage-Attendants_R-68808-1": {
      "title": "Casual Food and Beverage Attendants",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nCasual\n\nCustoms House – 399 Queen Street Award-winnin g venue – Best Wedding Caterer of QLD and NT on the Restaurant & Catering Awards for Excellence 2026 $39.12 per hour + 12% superannuation contributions Based at the heart of Brisbane City About This Opportunity  Bring memorable events and dining experiences to life at Customs House, one of Brisbane’s distinctive heritage hospitality venues. We are seeking energetic, polished and customer-focused Food and Beverage Attendants to join our collaborative team across Banquets Operations and Patina Restaurant. You will work in an inclusive, high-performing environment where professional service, teamwork and attention to detail create exceptional experiences for every guest. Staff must be available to work across a seven-day roster, including early mornings, evenings, late nights and weekends. Key responsibilities will include: Deliver professional food and beverage service and create a warm, memorable experience for guests. Set up and pack down restaurant, function and bar spaces, including equipment and furniture. Provide plate, clearing and tray service across banquets, events and restaurant operations. Prepare and serve beverages, including mixed drinks and cocktails, while applying sound wine knowledge. Support stock control, storage, presentation and the cleanliness of front-of-house and back-of-house areas. Communicate clearly during service and contribute to smooth handovers and collaborative teamwork. About You You will bring: A current Queensland Responsible Service of Alcohol certificate. Approximately two years of experience in a comparable food and beverage, restaurant, events or premium hospitality role. A genuine commitment to creating positive guest experiences through attentive and professional service. Experience with plate carrying, plate clearing and tray service. Confidence serving beverages, with cocktail-making skills and wine knowledge highly regarded. A positive, dependable and customer-focused approach. Strong communication and teamwork skills, with the ability to contribute during busy service periods. Flexibility to work across a seven-day roster, including early mornings, evenings, late nights and weekends. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a purposeful and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies. At Customs House, you will be part of a supportive team that values your hospitality experience and offers opportunities to learn, connect and grow. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Ms Carolina Muramoto, Banquet/Event Manager, at c.muramoto@customshouse.com.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload: A resume. Evidence of your current Queensland Responsible Service of Alcohol certificate. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position. Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. People with criminal records are not automatically barred from applying, and each application will be considered on its merits. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Please let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. If you are a current UQ employee, including a casual staff member or HDR scholar, or hold an unpaid or affiliate appointment, please sign in to your staff Workday account and apply through the internal careers board. Please do not apply through the external job board. Applications close Wednesday 14 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-66808). Applications will be reviewed as received, and candidates may be interviewed before the closing date. We encourage you to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close the application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---Institute-of-Modern-Languages_R-69033": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - Institute of Modern Languages",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-04\nPosted: Posted 10 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity      The University of Queensland’s Institute of Modern Languages is seeking expressions of interest from casual academics to be engaged on a casual basis.   Casual Academics make a rich and important contribution to the Institutes ’ language teaching and community engagement endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):    Teaching    Personalised Tutoring    Development of teaching materials   Exam Marking   Preparation of lesson plans   Report writing and other associated duties.   What We Can Offer      These are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.     For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) .     Eligibility Qualifications   A Bachelor level degree or other qualification in teaching, education, TESOL, languages or linguistics . Or a certificate IV in Training and Assessment or equivalent.   Knowledge and Skills   Native speaker or native-like proficiency in the target language.   Ability to develop programs for specific language learning p urposes .   Ability to evaluate & assess student progress.   Knowledge of current practices in language teaching methodology .   Experience   General or Language teaching experience.   Personal qualities   Good communication and interpersonal skills.   Ability to adopt a flexible approach to teaching style in response to student needs   You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. UQ’s Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to casualrecruit@uq.edu.au .     Want to Apply?     All applicants will be asked to nominate their course and/or general subject area preference/s in the application form. In addition, all applicants must upload the following documents for an application to be considered:   A copy of your current CV/resume   A brief statement addressing:   experience in tutoring/facilitating /marking exams ; experience using online tools (Padlet, Zoom, Teams); knowledge/expertise in the target language.  Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box during the application form. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board.   Questions?    To discuss the role please contact the [ Pru Rolff ] via email [ p.rolff@uq.edu.au ]     For technical queries about your application, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number   in the subject line."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Neuroscience_R-69089-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Neuroscience",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nQueensland Brain Institute Full-time (100% FTE), fixed-term position for up to 12 months commencing ASAP Base salary will range from $85,373 to $113,660 (Academic Level A) + 17% Super Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity We are seeking a motivated and driven Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join our world-leading Queensland Brain Institute. In this research-focused role , you will contribute to the development of novel monoclonal antibody therapies for Alzheimer’s disease. Working within a collaborative and high-performing neuroscience team, you will design and conduct in vitro and in vivo studies to validate antibodies targeting tau and help translate innovative discoveries into therapeutic outcomes. Join a vibrant research community of leading scientists, researchers and students dedicated to advancing neuroscience and creating meaningful impact through world-class discovery and translation. Key responsibilities will include: Research: Design and conduct research to improve and functionally validate antibodies targeting tau in neurodegeneration in vitro and in mouse models, undertake data analysis, prepare manuscripts, publish high-quality research and present findings at laboratory, institutional, national and international meetings, and prepare reports for the funders. Funding and Research Development: Apply for independent and collaborative research funding, maintain familiarity with relevant scientific literature and contribute actively to the disciplined academic environment of the laboratory and institute. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of junior researchers and students, support responsible research practices and help enable the smooth running of the laboratory. Citizenship and Service: Contribute to a safe, inclusive and collegial laboratory environment, participate in priority activities for the laboratory and institute, and uphold University values, policies and responsible research practices. About You Completion of a PhD in Biochemistry, Protein Engineering, Neuroscience or any relevant discipline. Demonstrated experience in protein chemistry, recombinant protein expression and cell culture. Demonstrated proficiency in oral and written communication. Publication(s) in reputable refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Experience contributing to meaningful internal service activities, collaborative research environments or external engagement. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Jürgen Götz, NHMRC Leadership Fellow, at j.goetz@uq.edu.au or Dr Esteban Cruz, antibody development leader e.cruz@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69089. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter including responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of the right to work in Australia and your qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close 13th October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69089)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Data-Science_R-68823": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Data Science",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFrazer Institute Full-time, fixed-term position until December 2028   Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% super (Academic Level A)  Based at our  Dutton Park Campus  About This Opportunity    Join a visionary and collaborative research team driving world-leading advances in melanoma and skin cancer research. Working at the intersection of data science, artificial intelligence, medical imaging, and clinical research, you will play a pivotal role in transforming complex research data into meaningful discoveries that have the potential to improve health outcomes globally. As a highly skilled Data Scientist, you will work alongside leading researchers, clinicians, AI specialists, and technical experts, leveraging cutting-edge technologies and world-class research infrastructure to develop innovative analytical solutions, enhance research capability, and help create lasting impact through data-driven discovery. At UQ, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while contributing to research that is part of something bigger, creating change together. Key responsibilities will include :    Research:  Design, develop and optimise clinical and skin imaging data management systems, workflows and research infrastructure. Apply advanced statistical, machine learning and data engineering methodologies to large-scale, longitudinal datasets, contributing to innovative melanoma and skin cancer research outcomes. Research Data Management and Analysis:  Lead the processing, cleaning, integration, validation and analysis of complex clinical and skin imaging datasets, including 2D and 3D medical imaging data. Develop, maintain and deploy robust, version-controlled analytical codebases and data solutions that support high-quality, reproducible research. Collaboration and Stakeholder Engagement:  Collaborate with researchers, clinicians, AI specialists and technical experts to enhance data platforms, modelling capability and analytical approaches. Provide specialist technical expertise, contribute to multidisciplinary research projects, and support continuous improvement across research systems and processes. Supervision and Researcher Development:  Train, mentor and support junior staff in data cleaning, quality assurance, data management and analytical best practices. Foster capability development and contribute to a collaborative and high-performing research environment. This is a  research focused  position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You    Completion of a PhD in Data Science, Biostatistics, Computer Science, Health Informatics, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated experience working with large, complex and longitudinal research datasets, including data cleaning, preprocessing, integration and quality assurance to support high-quality research outcomes. Proven expertise developing, validating and deploying advanced statistical and machine learning models using Python, R, or similar programming languages in a research environment. Experience working with melanoma, skin cancer or medical imaging research, including the analysis and management of 2D and 3D imaging datasets. Strong technical knowledge across the data lifecycle, including research data architecture, data pipelines, database management, SQL, REDCap, cloud or high-performance computing environments, and reproducible research practices. Excellent communication, stakeholder engagement and collaboration skills, with the ability to work effectively across multidisciplinary teams and provide training, mentoring and technical guidance to researchers and staff. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Caitlin Horsham at c.horsham@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.       Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.    Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in  Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close 14th of October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68823).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Nonequilibrium-Dynamics-in-Quantum-Computing_R-68679-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Nonequilibrium Dynamics in Quantum Computing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our vibrant and picturesque St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join a growing, collaborative research team developing innovative quantum algorithms for decarbonisation applications. Working within the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology and the Queensland Quantum Decarbonisation Alliance, your research will focus on dissipative state preparation for molecular systems and molecular dynamics. You will contribute to advancements in the use of nonequilibrium dynamics in quantum algorithms for applications in decarbonisation. Positioned at the intersection of fundamental discovery and real-world impact, the role offers opportunities to collaborate with quantum computing companies, application-domain partners, Queensland research institutions, and established experts in computational molecular and materials science and nonequilibrium statistical mechanics. Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Develop a coherent, quality research program focused on nonequilibrium dynamics, dissipative state preparation, and quantum algorithms for molecular systems, producing quality research outputs in high-quality outlets; Work with academic and industry partners to develop joint projects, pursue competitive research funding, and progress the translation of knowledge, technology, and practices to research end users. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Undergraduate Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, including feedback, coaching, and professional development. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through purposeful collaboration, participation in priority activities, support for academic colleagues and unit operations, and the development of relationships with industry, government, professional bodies, and the wider community. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in physics, mathematics, chemistry, computer science, engineering, or a closely related field. Strong foundational knowledge of, and research experience in, dissipative quantum dynamics. An emerging research profile in the dynamics of open quantum systems, quantum computing, and/or resource analysis of algorithms for fault-tolerant quantum computers. Experience in nonequilibrium statistical mechanics, molecular modelling, and/or quantum thermodynamics would also be beneficial. Evidence of quality publications in reputable refereed journals and experience presenting at conferences. Evidence of contributing to applications for external research funding or other mechanisms of research support. The capacity to collaborate effectively across disciplines and engage constructively with academic, government, and industry partners. The ability to contribute to student supervision, researcher development, internal service, and external engagement activities. About Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) The Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) creates solutions for some of society’s most serious problems in health, energy and the bioeconomy. Urgent solutions are needed for the way our global community generates and uses energy, engages with our environment, and deals with serious health issues. At the AIBN we're working on the answers. Our 400-strong team works closely with industry to translate cutting-edge science into practical solutions to help shift global reliance on fossil fuels; create healthier communities; build stronger, more sustainable economies; and create a more sustainable footprint overall. About Queensland Quantum Decarbonisation Alliance (QDA) The QDA brings together twenty-seven partners across academia and industry to create a world-leading decarbonisation effort in Queensland powered by quantum technologies. Led by UQ, it has the partnerships needed for success, including Griffith University, CSIRO and a range of industry partners including Siemens, Aurizon, Queensland Energy, among others. A key partner in the alliance is PsiQuantum, a leading quantum computing company and their decarbonisation affiliate Qlimate. PsiQuantum is currently building a utility-scale quantum computer in Brisbane. The QDA aims to pioneer the use of quantum computers for high-value decarbonisation projects such as improved battery chemistries, the development of new catalysts for hydrogen, biofuel or ammonia production, materials for hydrogen or carbon storage, or process optimisation in carbon-intensive industry. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Stephen Sanderson -   stephen.sanderson@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close 16 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68679)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Head-of-School--School-of-Mechanical-and-Mining-Engineering_R-65984-2": {
      "title": "Head of School, School of Mechanical and Mining Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Mechanical and Mining Engineering Full-time, 5-year fixed-term position at Academic Level E, with underlying continuing (permanent) Professorial position Based at our beautiful  St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity Lead one of the world's most respected engineering schools and help shape the future of engineering education, research and industry impact. The School of Mechanical and Mining Engineering is entering an exciting new chapter. With a strong global reputation, significant student growth, world-leading research capability, and deep industry connections, the School is uniquely positioned to influence the future of mechanical, aerospace, biomedical, materials, mechatronic and mining engineering. As Head of School, you will inherit a thriving, collaborative and inclusive community and be empowered to drive its next phase of growth. This is a rare opportunity to build on an extraordinary legacy while shaping the ideas, partnerships and people that will solve tomorrow's most complex challenges. The School’s commitment to research excellence and real-world impact is reflected in the outstanding global standing of UQ's Mineral and Mining Engineering discipline, ranked 5th in the world in the 2026 QS World University Rankings by Subject . In this role, you will: Shape the strategic direction of one of Australia's leading engineering schools. Lead a community of internationally recognised researchers, educators, professional staff and industry collaborators. Drive research excellence across areas transforming the future, including advanced manufacturing, renewable energy, robotics, automation, biomedical engineering and mining innovation. Build bold interdisciplinary partnerships that translate discovery into meaningful impact. Strengthen engagement with industry, government and global partners to create new opportunities for growth, influence and collaboration. Elevate the School's global reputation while protecting the collegial, supportive culture that makes it distinctive. Guide strategic investment decisions across people, infrastructure, education and research. Leave a lasting legacy through leadership that creates impact well beyond the University. For more information about this opportunity, please view the Appointment Booklet. About You We are seeking a respected academic leader with an international reputation for excellence and a passion for shaping the future of research, education and industry impact. Combining intellectual leadership with strategic vision, you will bring the credibility to influence at the highest levels and the ability to unite people around a shared ambition. Our ideal candidate will demonstrate: An internationally recognised reputation for academic excellence , with the credibility, leadership capability and strategic vision to enhance the standing and influence of the School and Faculty. A proven track record of leading and developing high-performing teams , creating a culture of collaboration, innovation and excellence, and inspiring others to achieve ambitious goals in research, teaching and engagement. Exceptional stakeholder engagement, influencing and relationship-building skills , with the ability to forge productive partnerships across industry, government, professional bodies and the broader community. Strong strategic, operational and commercial acumen , including experience leading complex organisations and managing financial, human and physical resources to deliver sustainable growth and impact. A commitment to inclusive leadership and organisational stewardship , demonstrated through fostering equity, diversity and inclusion, supporting staff development and wellbeing, and navigating governance, policy and regulatory responsibilities with integrity. Most importantly, you are excited by the opportunity to lead a community with extraordinary potential and help define its next chapter. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. At the core of our teaching remains our students, and their experience with us sets a foundation for success far beyond graduation. UQ has made a commitment to making education opportunities available for all Queenslanders, regardless of personal, financial, or geographical barriers. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. Interested? If you'd like to have a confidential discussion, please contact Caylee-Jade Eve, Principal Consultant, Executive Recruitment via executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au .  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the 'About You' section in the attached position booklet . Other Information Appointment to this role is subject to the successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, which will include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package. You can find out more about life in  Australia’s Sunshine State here .  We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We're also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday the 26th October at 11.00pm AEST ( R-65984 )."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow---Nutrition-and-Dietetics_R-67941-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow – Nutrition and Dietetics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Human Movement and Nutrition Sciences Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to two years Base salary will be in the range $119,462 to $141,545 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Based at our vibrant St Lucia campus About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and inclusive academic community advancing public health nutrition through purposeful research, teaching and engagement. Working alongside academics in the School of Human Movement and Nutrition Sciences, you will develop an independent research profile, contribute to nationally and internationally recognised research, engage with policy and practice stakeholders, and translate evidence into meaningful outcomes for communities. Key responsibilities will include. Research: Develop an independent and team-based research program in public health nutrition, including nutritional epidemiology, population-based dietary interventions, nutrition promotion, and food and nutrition policy. Publish high-quality research, pursue external funding, apply rigorous quantitative and qualitative methods, and translate evidence to inform policy and practice. Teaching: Develop and deliver undergraduate and postgraduate teaching across different settings, contribute to innovative curriculum design, coordinate courses, and undertake assessment and marking in accordance with University requirements. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Master of Dietetics Studies research project students, Honours students, and Higher Degree by Research candidates. Support researcher capability, positive working relationships, and effective employee processes in accordance with University policies. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships with colleagues, government, industry, professional bodies, and the wider community. Contribute to governance, committees and internal service, while demonstrating leadership through mentoring, collaboration, and the consistent application of UQ values. This is a  research focused  position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion of a PhD in nutrition, dietetics, public health, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated research experience in population nutrition, behavioural nutrition, nutritional epidemiology, food policy, or another area of public health nutrition. A strong track record of publications in respected peer-reviewed journals and presentations at conferences, relative to career stage. Evidence of national standing and an emerging international profile in public health nutrition. Experience applying quantitative research methods to large datasets using advanced statistical methods and software such as R, Stata, or SAS. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining, or managing external research funding. Experience engaging effectively with government, community, policy, or practice stakeholders and working collaboratively within interdisciplinary teams. A growing record of student supervision, high-quality university teaching, academic service, and external engagement, supported by excellent communication, organisational, and project management skills. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. At UQ, you will be part of a visionary and connected research community committed to discovery, translation, and impact. You will be supported to bring together diverse perspectives, challenge conventional thinking, and create meaningful change in nutrition policy, practice, and population health. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Sarah McNaughton at s.mcnaughton@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 15 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67941)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Architectural-Technology_R-68599-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Architectural Technology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Architecture, Design and Planning – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years commencing January 2027 Base salary will be in the range $145,961.51 - $168,043.68 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Visa sponsorship may be available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  We are seeking a passionate and experienced Senior Lecturer in Architectural Technology to join our vibrant academic community at the School of Architecture, Design and Planning (ADP) at our beautiful St Lucia campus. Shape the future of architecture and digital design at UQ and be part of a university consistently ranked in the world’s top 40. This exciting new role combines industry expertise with teaching, research and collaboration to inspire future design leaders and drive innovation. You will play a key role in advancing architectural technology, digital design, sustainability and AI across UQ's teaching and research programs. Collaborating with colleagues and industry, you will help equip graduates to tackle future built environment challenges. We welcome applicants who may prefer a different service fraction—please indicate your preferred arrangement in your cover letter. Flexible options, including job share, may be considered to accommodate the right candidate. If you are ready to make your mark in a world-class institution that invests in its people and their potential, we would love to hear from you. Key responsibilities will include:  Inspire through teaching – Shape the future of architecture with engaging, innovative courses, lectures and tutorials that connect theory to real-world practice, particularly in mainstreams of architectural technology and architectural science. This is expected to happen through leading design studios that connect deep architectural theory with computation, fabrication, performance and material experimentation. Lead impactful research – Build a strong research profile, publish in leading international journals, and secure funding to drive change across the built environment sector and in collaboration with other sectors/disciplines. Mentor future leaders – Guide and support Honours and Higher Degree by Research students to thrive as the next generation of architectural designers/scientists and design innovators. Engage and connect – Collaborate with colleagues, industry, government and the wider community while contributing to UQ’s vibrant academic culture. Drive technological innovation – Advance the School's strategic priorities in architectural technology by developing and integrating digital design methodologies, computational design, building performance analysis, digital fabrication, AI-assisted design, sustainable construction, and emerging technologies into teaching, research and industry engagement. In doing so, the successful candidate is expected to develop and critically evaluate bespoke AI and computational tools, rather than only the use of existing platforms. This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  A PhD in Architecture, Architectural Science, Architectural Technology, or a related discipline. An established profile and leadership in teaching and research in architecture, architectural technology or architectural science.  Evidence of reputation and track record of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences.  Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing internal and external research funding.  Evidence of a high level of quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate level across a variety of settings, including small and large groups/classrooms.  Experience in course coordination and contribution to the development and continuous innovation of new programs, curriculum design and course material in the discipline area.  Technologically proficient and demonstrated leadership in integrating relevant digital and technical methods into curriculum, including critique of bias, authorship, automation and contextual relevance. Technically skilled and experienced, with a strong understanding of construction materials, systems and processes and their impact on documentation and detailing. An evidenced record of supervision of Honours and Research Higher Degree students to successful completion.  Experience in meaningful internal service roles in conjunction with active contributions to external activities.  We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Ali Cheshmehzangi (Head of School) at a.chesh@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday, 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68599)."
