    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Power-Systems-Stability-and-Oscillation-Localisation_R-69503-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Power Systems Stability and Oscillation Localisation",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time fixed-term position through to end of March 2029 Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72+ 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a visionary and collaborative research team working at the forefront of Australia’s energy transition. As a Postdoctoral Research Fellow, you will contribute to the Grid Enhancement Technologies grants program funded by the Department of Climate Change, Energy, the Environment and Water for the project, “Enhancing Grid Stability: Oscillation Localisation and Visualisation”, through a partnership between  EPEC Engineering, the University of Queensland and Powerlink Queensland.   Working within the Power, Energy and Control Engineering discipline in the School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, you will help develop innovative solutions that improve the stability, visibility and resilience of Australia’s electricity network. Leveraging world-class facilities and a connected network of industry and research partners, you will play a meaningful role in accelerating secure renewable energy integration and supporting Australia’s net-zero ambitions.   Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Develop advanced oscillation source localisation techniques for inverter-based resource dominated power systems, including mode identification, source detection, dynamic event classification, hardware-in-the-loop validation, research publication, and grant-supported project delivery. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree Research students, support researcher capability development, and collaborate in the delivery of professional development initiatives. Citizenship and Service:  Foster productive partnerships with industry and government stakeholders, contribute to School and University initiatives, participate in service activities, and demonstrate behaviours aligned with UQ values. Industry Engagement: Work closely with EPEC Engineering, Powerlink Queensland, and other stakeholders to translate research outcomes into practical tools that strengthen network operations and renewable energy integration.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .     About You  You are an emerging researcher who is passionate about solving complex energy challenges and creating meaningful impact through innovative power systems research. You will bring: Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in Electrical Engineering or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in power system dynamics, stability analysis, renewable energy integration, or related areas. Knowledge of data-driven analytics, machine learning, signal processing, or advanced modelling techniques relevant to power systems. Experience with real-time simulation platforms, hardware-in-the-loop testing, or power system validation methodologies. Evidence of publication in reputable peer-reviewed journals and presentation of research outcomes at conferences. An emerging research profile with the ability to contribute to collaborative, externally funded research projects. Strong interpersonal and communication skills with the ability to engage effectively with researchers, students, industry partners and government stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to fostering an inclusive, respectful and collaborative research environment.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Feifei Bai ( f.bai@uq.edu.au ) . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Thursday October 22nd 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69503). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for early November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Scientific-Officer---Materials-Characterisation---Cleanroom-Operations_R-69206-1": {
      "title": "Scientific Officer – Materials Characterisation & Cleanroom Operations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nAustralian National Fabrication Facility, Queensland Node (ANFF-Qld) , based at the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to 30 June 2028. Base salary will be in the range $106,709.01 to $116,198.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  The Australian National Fabrication Facility, Queensland Node (ANFF-Qld) is seeking an experienced and collaborative Scientific Officer to strengthen its materials characterisation capability and support safe, reliable cleanroom operations. Working within a connected research infrastructure team, you will take technical ownership of specialist equipment, enable researchers and industry users to achieve high-quality outcomes, and contribute to an inclusive environment where expertise is shared and new capabilities are developed together.   Reporting to the ANFF-Qld Facility Manager, the position will have two primary areas of responsibility: Approximately 60% – Materials Characterisation Technical Ownership: Primary ownership and operation of assigned materials characterisation tools and systems, including user training, calibration, preventative maintenance, troubleshooting, technical support and capability development.   Approximately 40% – Cleanroom Operational Support: Supporting effective day-to-day cleanroom operations, including chemicals, PPE, consumables, inventory, chemical waste and maintaining a safe, clean and well-organised laboratory environment.   The position will work closely with ANFF-Qld technical staff, AIBN infrastructure teams, researchers, students and industry users   Key responsibilities will include:    Act as primary technical owner for assigned materials characterisation tools and systems, including GPC, FTIR and other relevant analytical or characterisation techniques, based on facility operational requirements. Manage the full operational lifecycle of assigned equipment, including user access, training and competency assessment, calibration, preventative maintenance, troubleshooting, service coordination, performance monitoring and equipment reliability. Provide specialist technical support and consultation to researchers and industry users, including sample preparation, measurement methodology, data quality and appropriate use of characterisation techniques. Develop, maintain and continuously improve SOPs, risk assessments, training materials, equipment records and quality documentation for assigned tools and technical processes. Contribute to the development, optimisation and implementation of new characterisation methodologies, processes and technical capabilities, and support knowledge transfer across the ANFF-Qld team. Support cleanroom operations by maintaining appropriate stocks of chemicals, PPE, laboratory materials and consumables, including purchasing, receiving, restocking, inventory monitoring and usage records through relevant UQ systems. Coordinate the safe storage, handling and disposal of cleanroom chemicals and chemical waste in accordance with UQ, AIBN and facility safety requirements and procedures. Maintain a high standard of cleanroom organisation and operational readiness, including PPE stations, consumable storage, chemical cabinets, sticky mats and other routine cleanroom requirements. Serve as primary, joint or backup technical owner for other relevant characterisation, imaging, metrology or micro/nanofabrication equipment where required or as assigned by Facility Manger. Contribute to RIMS administration, operational reporting, safety, quality and continuous improvement activities. Work collaboratively with ANFF-Qld and AIBN staff, researchers, students, industry clients and other stakeholders, and undertake other reasonable technical or operational duties consistent with the scope and work level of the position and ANFF-Qld operational requirements.   About You You will have:   A tertiary qualification in chemistry, materials science, physics, engineering or a closely related discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and professional experience. Demonstrated hands-on experience working with materials characterisation, analytical or metrology equipment, preferably within a research infrastructure, and university, biomedical, semiconductor, advanced manufacturing or similar technical environment. Practical experience with one or more relevant characterisation techniques such as GPC, FTIR, spectroscopy, microscopy, surface characterisation or related materials analysis techniques. Demonstrated ability to take technical ownership of scientific equipment, including operation, calibration, preventative maintenance, troubleshooting, fault diagnosis and coordination with equipment suppliers or service providers. Experience providing technical training, user onboarding and competency assessment, with the ability to communicate equipment operation, scientific methods and safety requirements clearly to users with different levels of experience. Demonstrated experience working safely in a cleanroom, PC2 laboratory or controlled laboratory environment, with sound knowledge of laboratory safety, chemical handling, risk assessment and waste-management requirements. Experience managing laboratory or cleanroom chemicals, consumables, PPE and inventory, including purchasing, stock monitoring, storage and replenishment. Demonstrated ability to develop and maintain high-quality technical documentation, including SOPs, risk assessments, equipment records, maintenance documentation and training materials. Strong analytical and problem-solving capability, with the ability to independently identify equipment or process issues, determine appropriate actions and escalate technical or safety risks when required. Strong interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with technical colleagues, researchers, students, industry users and other internal and external stakeholders.   About the Australian National Fabrication Facility (ANFF-Qld) ANFF-Qld is a not-for-profit research infrastructure facility established under the National Collaborative Research Infrastructure Strategy (NCRIS). ANFF is a network of eight nodes distributed across Australia, supported by over $200 million in investment in equipment, infrastructure, and personnel.   The Queensland Node (ANFF-Qld) is a state-of-the-art fabrication facility specialising in microfluidics, organic electronics and optoelectronics, biomaterials, semiconductor, quantum and photonic technologies, novel materials, and advanced imaging and characterisation.   Its facilities are located within the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) and the Centre for Organic Photonics & Electronics (COPE) at The University of Queensland, as well as the Queensland Microtechnology Facility (QMF) at Griffith University. ANFF-Qld also facilitates access to other ANFF sites and the broader national network across Australian universities.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Ethan Aung - anffq.manager@uq.edu.au   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, background checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69206). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 27 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Supramolecular-Chemistry_R-68675-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Supramolecular Chemistry",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nSchool of Chemistry and Molecular Biosciences Full-time (100%), fixed term position for up to 2 years   Base salary will be in the range $85,372 to $113,659 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia location     About This Opportunity    Join a collaborative and impact-driven research environment and contribute to an Australian Research Council funded project investigating supramolecular chemistry and flexibility in molecular crystals. You will undertake original research spanning molecular synthesis, crystal growth and advanced characterisation, while building experience in diffraction techniques, including work at major facilities. Supported by a connected community of researchers, you will be encouraged to contribute ideas, develop your research profile and help create meaningful change through globally relevant discovery.   Key responsibilities will include:   Research: Conduct high quality, original and collaborative research in supramolecular chemistry, with a particular focus on flexible molecular crystals. Undertake molecular synthesis, crystal growth and advanced characterisation, develop and implement diffraction methods, including at major facilities, and communicate findings through high-quality publications, reports, seminars and conference presentations. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, as appropriate, while supporting a responsible and inclusive research culture. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through collaboration, active participation in priority activities, internal service and engagement, and the responsible implementation of University policies and procedures.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .     About You      Essential Completion or near completion of a PhD in a relevant area of chemistry. An emerging profile in research in the discipline area. Demonstrated expertise in the synthesis and purification of molecules, ideally related to the field of supramolecular chemistry, as well as experience in advanced molecular characterisation methods. Demonstrated capability to produce, publish and communicate high quality research through publications in refereed journals and presentations at conferences. Proven success at working collaboratively and effectively in a high-performing setting. Evidence of contributions towards successfully obtaining external research funding.   We recognise that candidates may bring different strengths and experiences. The following criteria are desirable rather than essential, and we encourage you to highlight any relevant experience in your application:   Single crystal diffraction and advanced diffraction methods. Density functional theory calculations. Python programming, or a willingness to learn. Higher degree student supervision. Strong initiative, resourcefulness and the ability to work effectively to deadlines.   About UQ    As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.     The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.     Interested?    For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Jack Clegg at j.clegg@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter addressing the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information     Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close 14 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R- 68675). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for early in the week commencing 26 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Research-Fellow--ZeroPC_R-68319-1": {
      "title": "Research Fellow, ZeroPC",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-21\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), fixed-term position through to October 2028 Base salary will be in the range $106,294 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity to join an established, expert team to conduct innovative research on thermoelectric materials for low-grade waste heat recovery. Working on a project within the ARC Research Hub in Zero-Emission Power Generation for Carbon Neutrality, known as ZeroPC, you will collaborate with researchers and industry partners across UQ, QUT and HBIS Group.   A high level of knowledge, expertise and experience in thermoelectric materials and devices will be required for this project, as you will be expected to carry out experimental and computational studies on thermoelectric materials, as well as supporting the supervision and development of Undergraduate, Honours and Higher Degree Research students.   Key responsibilities will include:    Research: Produce high-quality research outputs and work within a cross-disciplinary team to fabricate, characterise and test thermoelectric materials, thermoelectric generators and integrated waste-heat recovery systems; Undertake density functional theory calculations and finite element analysis to guide the selection and development of thermoelectric materials and devices, while contributing to funding applications and research translation.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Support the effective supervision and development of undergraduate project, Honours and Higher Degree by Research students through feedback, coaching and collaborative research practice.   Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values, contribute to priority activities and internal service, and help develop productive relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You    Completion or near completion of a PhD in the fields of chemistry, chemical engineering, materials engineering, physics or similar field; Demonstrated expertise in fabrication and characterisation of thermoelectric materials and thermoelectric generators; Demonstrate expertise in DFT calculations or or finite element analysis of thermoelectric materials and devices; Evidence of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Evidence of contributions towards successfully obtaining external research funding; Ability to work well in a team and to contribute to development of a collaborative and supportive research environment. About ZeroPC ZeroPC is an ARC Hub and partnership between University and Industry aiming to develop sustainable zero-emission power generation technologies to transform gaseous waste from our energy and manufacturing sectors into valuable products and create scalable pathways to market for driving industry transformation. This Hub plans to harvest renewable energy from the environment by using zero-emission thermoelectric power generators and then store it in green and safer batteries for converting gaseous waste from sectors that cannot easily avoid emission into useful chemicals, which in turn realize carbon neutrality and negativity .   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Prof Debra Bernhardt - d.bernhardt@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68319)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Primary-Care-Veterinary-Clinician_R-69194-1": {
      "title": "Primary Care Veterinary Clinician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nUQ VETS . Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Join our Gatton Campus community – take a virtual tour !   About This Opportunity We are seeking a motivated and energetic Veterinary Clinician to join the UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital at the UQ Gatton Veterinary Teaching Hospital. Working within a collaborative, inquisitive and purpose-driven clinical environment, this role supports the delivery of high-quality, relationship-centred veterinary care while contributing to the education and mentoring of the next generation of veterinarians and veterinary technologists. As part of a visionary and supportive team, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while helping create meaningful change for animals, clients, students and the veterinary profession.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Provide high-quality, relationship-centred clinical care to animal patients and their owners, independently managing both routine and complex veterinary cases. Participate in the management of primary care, urgent and emergency cases within the Small Animal Hospital. Actively contribute to clinical teaching, instruction and supervision of undergraduate veterinary science and veterinary technology students, as well as mentoring interns and junior clinicians. Support continuous improvement initiatives, clinical leadership, and effective day-to-day hospital operations in alignment with University and professional standards.   About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders.   Instead of solely concentrating on meeting caseload benchmarks or revenue targets, your priorities lie in creating an impact, establishing a lasting impression on the field, and conducting work that brings about positive changes in the lives of animals.   The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Hospital is a largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest in veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a 7-day urgent and emergency care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus, Wacol.   In particular, the UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital values every animal and offers unrivalled medical, surgical and diagnostic services for dogs, cats, birds, reptiles, small mammals and fish at our world-class veterinary hospital in Gatton.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:   Free parking and intercampus bus. On campus childcare, medical clinic, and post office. Up to 26 weeks paid parental leave/paid primary care leave. 17% superannuation contributions + 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts: fitness passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance, and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities: access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program. 25% discount on eligible UQ Vets services. Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sports facility.   About You You will bring professional expertise, enthusiasm for teaching, and a collaborative mindset to this role.   A Bachelor of Veterinary Science or equivalent qualification, registrable in Queensland. More than four years’ experience in general practice or emergency and critical care. Membership of ANZCVS or an equivalent qualification is desirable, with a demonstrated commitment to ongoing professional development. Demonstrated commitment to relationship-centred veterinary care and teamwork across clinical and teaching environments. Proven ability to manage complex clinical cases and provide mentoring and clinical leadership to junior staff. Strong commitment to teaching and training veterinary science and veterinary technology students. Sound knowledge of legislative, professional and workplace health and safety requirements relevant to veterinary practice in Queensland. Highly developed interpersonal, communication, teamwork and consultation skills, with the ability to represent the School to industry, government and the wider community.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Wendy Goodwin w.goodwin@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, background checks, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69194)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/University-Centre---Toowoomba/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R-68718-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nRural Clinical School Full-time, fixed-term position for 12 months Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% super (Academic Level A)  Based at our Toowoomba Regional Clinical Unit    About This Opportunity    Join a collaborative and purpose-driven research team dedicated to improving the health and wellbeing of regional, rural and remote Australians! This exciting Postdoctoral Research Fellow – Rural Health opportunity will enable you to build your research profile while contributing to impactful projects that address some of the most pressing healthcare challenges facing rural communities. Working alongside leading researchers in a supportive, inclusive and innovative environment, you will play a key role in advancing high-quality mixed-methods research, fostering meaningful partnerships, and translating research outcomes into positive change for communities. At UQ, you will be challenged, inspired and rewarded while working with like-minded colleagues who share a vision of creating change together.  Key responsibilities will include:     Research: Lead and contribute to high-quality mixed-methods research projects, publish in reputable peer-reviewed journals, present research findings at conferences, collaborate on interdisciplinary research initiatives, support the development of competitive research funding applications, and contribute to research that improves the health and wellbeing of regional, rural and remote Australians. Supervision and Researcher Development: Support the supervision of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, contribute to researcher development activities, provide mentoring and guidance to emerging researchers, foster a positive and collaborative research culture, and demonstrate personal effectiveness in supervision and research capacity building. Citizenship and Service: Develactivities and productive partnerships with internal and external stakeholders, contribute to service and engagement activities, provide support and mentorship to colleagues, participate in relevant committees and administrative activities, and uphold the values and strategic objectives of the University.  This is a  research focused  position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .     About You    Completion or near completion of a PhD in medicine, public health, rural health, or a related health discipline. Demonstrated experience leading, coordinating and contributing to high-quality non-lab-based research, including mixed-methods research projects. An emerging research profile in rural health, supported by publications in peer-reviewed journals and conference presentations. Evidence of contributing to competitive research funding applications and collaborative research initiatives. Strong interpersonal and organisational skills, with the ability to work effectively in a fast-paced, collaborative and supportive team environment. Understanding of, or experience working within, a rural healthcare or rural health workforce research setting, together with a current Queensland driver's licence.    About Rural Clinical School Located within the UQ Medical School, the Rural Clinical School (RCS) is the largest rural clinical school hosted by a single university. It works to foster and sustain a skilled and dynamic medical workforce to meet the needs of rural, regional, and remote Queensland. The Rural Clinical School provides students with rigorous clinical training built upon hands-on experiences in hospitals and community-based practices. Above all, we give our students the unique opportunity to live and work in some of Queensland’s amazing rural, regional, and remote locations. Our classrooms are the public and private hospitals, specialist consulting rooms, general practices and community healthcare settings throughout rural, regional and remote Queensland. Our teachers are dedicated and passionate rural clinicians. Our students get the opportunity to learn in a range of clinical contexts across the public, private and community sectors. The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.     Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Bushra Nasir | Senior Research Fellow at b.nasir@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.        Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.    We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Monday 19 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68718).  Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week of the 2nd November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Veterinary-Technician_R-68825-1": {
      "title": "Veterinary Technician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nUQ VETS . Part-time (50%), permanent position, commencing 4 January 2027. Base salary will be in the range $79,096 to $83,863 + 17% super (HEW Level 4). Join our Gatton Campus community and take a virtual tour .   About This Opportunity Join the purposeful and collaborative UQ VETS Clinical Studies Centre, where you will help coordinate the Teaching Laboratory and provide skilled operational, technical and instructional support for undergraduate practical teaching, continuing education workshops and seminars. Reporting to the Clinical Studies Centre Manager and working alongside a team of veterinary, technical and animal care professionals, you will contribute to high-quality animal care, an exceptional student experience and the development of the next generation of veterinary professionals.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Coordinate the daily operation of the Teaching Laboratory, class preparation, inventory management, stock rotation, ordering and routine maintenance. Provide professional medical, surgical and technical support, including animal handling and husbandry, anaesthesia and post-operative monitoring, basic laboratory procedures, diagnostic clinical pathology and assistance with specialised imaging procedures. Support practical teaching and continuing education activities by working with academic staff, assisting students and preparing animals, equipment and facilities for teaching classes and practical surgery. Assist with clipping and sorting out cadavers for practicals. Contribute to responsive and efficient Centre operations through equipment care, record keeping, customer service, administrative support and the Veterinary School Animal Adoption Program.   About UQ VETS A career at UQ VETS means making a difference to not only the lives of the people and animals in your care, but also the next generation of vets and vet technicians. At UQ VETS, we offer the opportunity to build a dynamic career through structured development programs and access to industry leaders.   The Veterinary Teaching Hospital is an organisational unit within the School that includes all of the School’s outstanding clinical facilities. UQ VETS Equine Hospital is a largely referral-based hospital with advanced facilities and offers a 24-hour emergency medical and surgical service. UQ VETS Small Animal Hospital boasts the latest veterinary medical and surgical diagnostic and treatment options for small animals, birds, exotic pets and wildlife, as well as a seven-day urgent and emergency care service. UQ staff also supervise clinical placements at the RSPCA’s Brisbane Animal Care Campus, Wacol.   In particular, the UQ VETS Clinical Studies Centre facilitates and supports pre-clinical and clinical training in companion animals and is a renowned research hub for clinical and behavioural research. The Centre includes a 16-table surgical teaching facility and is also used for veterinary continuing education events. The Clinical Studies Centre is also home to the School’s Pets-for-Life Adoption Program.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary, inclusive and collaborative community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as: Free parking and an intercampus bus . On-campus childcare , a medical clinic and a post office. Up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave or paid primary care leave. 17% super contributions and 17.5% annual leave loading. Health and wellness discounts, including Fitness Passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Career development opportunities, including access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and our Study for Staff program. A 25% discount on eligible UQ VETS services . Access to our $4.1 million UQ Sport facility .   UQ’s commitment to advancing animal health is reflected in research that broadens understanding of viruses affecting people and animals. Read about UQ researchers’ identification of the first henipavirus detected in North America and their work to identify better ways to protect against this virus family .   About You You will bring practical veterinary nursing or technical experience, sound judgement and a genuine commitment to animal welfare and student learning. You will have: Qualifications and training equivalent to a Certificate IV in Veterinary Nursing, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience, together with a current open driver licence. Knowledge and experience in low-stress animal husbandry, handling, restraint and care, particularly involving dogs, cats, birds, horses, pigs and sheep. Experience working with animals in a laboratory, teaching, technical or clinical environment, including the handling of cadavers and assisting with animal euthanasia. Knowledge and experience in companion animal behaviour, medical and surgical nursing, and the monitoring of anaesthesia, analgesia and post-operative recovery. Knowledge of the relevant codes, legislation and ethical requirements governing the use of animals in research and teaching, together with a strong commitment to animal welfare. Strong organisational and record-keeping skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands, work independently, use initiative and contribute to improved work practices. Effective interpersonal and customer service skills, with the ability to build cooperative relationships with veterinary professionals, academic staff, students, animal attendants, clients and other colleagues. Digital capability across word processing, databases, spreadsheets, email and practice management software, together with a commitment to UQ’s values of openness, respect and integrity.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Debra Laws d.laws@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68825)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/School-of-Social-Science--Casual-Academic-Opportunities_R-69676": {
      "title": "School of Social Science: Casual Academic Opportunities",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-25\nPosted: Posted Today\nCasual\n\nAbout This Opportunity  The School of Social Science is seeking Casual Academics for Summer Semester 2026/2027. Casual Academics make a rich and important contribution to the School’s academic, teaching and research endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. Casual Academics may be engaged to fulfil a wide range of duties, including (but not limited to) lecturing; tutoring; marking; teaching; moderating/facilitating and/or student advising; across multiple academic areas.  Casual Academics are paid according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement.   Casual Academic responsibilities may include (but are not limited to):  Lecturing/Teaching  Tutoring and Coordinating Tutoring  Assisting Course Coordinators  Marking  What We Can Offer  Current School of Social Science HDR students can apply to undertake casual academic positions for the courses listed on the School of Social Science casual academics webpage .  These are casual academic appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary depending on the type of work completed. Please view our various casual academic pay scales.    The marking rate for standard essays is 3,500 words per hour and the pay rate code is AC30A or AC30B.  For example, if marking 2,000 words for 18 students, then 2,000 x 18 / 3500 = 10 hours 17 mins. Initial marking hours are based upon expected enrolment figures, which often fluctuate in the first few weeks of semester. After census date, all marking is reassessed using census enrolment figures, which means that your allocated hours for marking may increase or decrease depending on student numbers.  For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).  Eligibility In the School of Social Science preference is given to our current HDR students and HDR students enrolled in the ISSR for employment opportunities. You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. UQ’s Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au . Want to Apply? All applicants will be asked to nominate their course and/or general subject area preference/s in the application form. In addition, all applicants must upload the following documents for an application to be considered: A copy of your current CV/resume A brief statement addressing: experience in tutoring/facilitating/marking undergraduate or postgraduate coursework; experience using online tools (Blackboard, and other digital tools, e.g., Zoom, Kaltura, or lecture recording software); and knowledge/expertise in the academic content of the course/s. Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box during the application form. The submission of your application is appreciated as soon as possible however is due no later than 11.00 pm AEST on Sunday 18 October 2026. If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Pro-Vice-Chancellor--Indigenous-Education---Research----Identified-s25_R-68552-1": {
      "title": "Pro Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Education & Research) - Identified s25",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nThe University of Queensland Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Education and Research) Full-time, 5 year Contract | St Lucia, Queensland| Senior Leadership Position The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The position is therefore only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people.   About the organisation The University of Queensland (UQ) is one of Australia’s most established and respected institutions, known for shaping ideas, advancing knowledge and contributing to positive change for more than a century. UQ offers a rich and contemporary learning experience, combining flexible study pathways, strong industry and community partnerships, and a vibrant campus culture across its major locations in South East Queensland. Students benefit from an environment that blends academic excellence with practical, real‑world learning, supported by a long‑standing commitment to high‑quality teaching and an inclusive, globally minded community.   UQ is recognised for its research leadership, with a network of faculties, institutes and centres that bring together experts across disciplines to address complex challenges and drive innovation. The University has a strong record of translating research into real‑world impact and maintains active engagement with partners across government, industry and communities. As a member of leading national and international university networks, UQ plays a significant role in shaping higher education and research collaboration globally. Its governance is overseen by a Senate that provides strategic stewardship and ensures the University continues to advance its mission and serve the broader community. This position sits within the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement), a University-wide division advancing UQ’s commitment to recognising Indigenous knowledges, connections to Country and respectful engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. Led by Professor Bronwyn Fredericks, the Division drives key priorities including the Reconciliation Action Plan, Indigenous employment pathways, student success, community partnerships and the integration of Indigenous perspectives across teaching, research and organisational practice. About the role The Pro‑Vice‑Chancellor (Indigenous Education and Research) is an accomplished Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander academic leader with Professorial‑level standing and strong community trust. The role requires deep cultural authority, a credible academic profile, and a demonstrated record of impact across Indigenous education, research and engagement. Working in close partnership with the Deputy Vice‑Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement), the PVC shapes whole‑of‑University priorities, guides major initiatives such as the Indigenous Curriculum framework, and contributes to senior governance forums with confidence and clarity.   Success in this position relies on collaborative leadership, cultural integrity and the ability to influence a complex academic environment. The PVC fosters meaningful relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and organisations, champions ethical and sustained partnerships, and strengthens pathways for Indigenous students, researchers and staff. The role calls for highly developed interpersonal and cross‑cultural capability, a steady and reliable presence in senior leadership, and a commitment to advancing reconciliation, Indigenous excellence and long‑term institutional change.   For a full list of responsibilities for the above role and to view the information booklet, please contact Pipeline Talent via jobs@pipelinetalent.com.au .   About you You will be an accomplished Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander academic leader with Professorial‑level standing, recognised for strong community trust, cultural authority and a demonstrated record of impact across Indigenous education, research and engagement.   Bringing credible academic expertise and a collaborative leadership style, you will help shape whole‑of‑University priorities alongside the Deputy Vice‑Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement), guide major initiatives such as the Indigenising Curriculum framework, and contribute meaningfully to senior governance forums. Steady, culturally grounded and influential through relationships rather than directive authority, you will foster ethical engagement with communities, strengthen pathways for Indigenous students, researchers and staff, and advance reconciliation, Indigenous excellence and long‑term institutional change across the University.   Selection Criteria Demonstrated academic leadership at Professorial level, including experience contributing to curriculum, research or governance in a university setting. Proven ability to influence change across a complex organisation, working through relationships, collaboration and cultural authority rather than directive power. Experience applying evidence‑informed and culturally grounded practices to strengthen Indigenous education, research or student success. Credibility to represent Indigenous priorities and perspectives across senior committees, faculties and external partners, with strong cultural integrity and community trust. Strong relationship‑management and communication skills, including cross‑cultural capability, negotiation, diplomacy and the ability to build alignment across diverse stakeholders.   What we can offer you 17% superannuation Cultural Support On-site childcare and school holiday programs Access to Employee Assistance Program  Generous annual leave, personal leave, parental and adoption leave, carer's leave and cultural leave Staff Discounts (including discounted on-campus parking)   How to apply Your application should include: A current resume. A cover letter responding to the selection criteria and outlining your suitability for the position.   Applications are to be submitted via the Pipeline Talent website at: https://www.pipelinetalent.com.au   Applications close 11:59pm Sunday, 25th of October 2026   How to get more information For further information on the position, please contact the team at Pipeline Talent. Dave Collins Director, Recruitment E: dave@pipelinetalent.com.au | jobs@pipelinetalent.com.au PH: 02 8001 6603"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer---Senior-Lecturer-in-Economics_R-69343-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer / Senior Lecturer in Economics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nSchool of Economics based at UQ’s Faculty of Business, Economics and Law (BEL) Two full-time (100%) permanent positions available Positions can be offered at Academic Level B or C: Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level B) Base salary will be in the range $145,961.51 - $168,043.68 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Based at UQ’s vibrant St Lucia Campus .   About This Opportunity  As a teaching and research academic Lecturer (Level B) or Senior Lecturer (Level C) in one of Australia’s leading economics schools, you will join a vibrant, internationally recognised academic community, committed to teaching excellence and international scholarly recognition in your field.   For US applicants: A Lecturer (Level B) is broadly comparable to an Assistant Professor in the US, while a Senior Lecturer (Level C) is a more senior appointment with greater expectations across research, teaching and academic leadership.   Key responsibilities at Academic Level B (Lecturer) will include:   Teaching: Contribute to the development of new programs and course materials in the area of focus, in consultation with program advisors and stakeholders, ensuring courses are engaging, relevant and contemporary. Teach across a range of settings and actively contribute to educational practice and innovative curriculum design, including online learning and alternative teaching methods. Coordinate courses, prepare and deliver lectures and tutorials, and undertake assessment and marking for undergraduate and postgraduate courses. Maintain and improve course quality, informed by evaluation tools, to meet industry and educational standards. Provide high-quality support to students, including academic counselling and advice. Understand and apply University rules relevant to teaching and learning practice.   Research: Develop a publication record in world-leading or internationally excellent journals in any field of Economics. Work with colleagues and postgraduate students to develop and conduct joint research projects and prepare applications for competitive research funding. Contribute to applications for external research funding and actively seek, secure and manage research funding. Present at key domestic and international conferences and other leading universities.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision of master’s, honours and higher degree by research students. Contribute to the recruitment, selection and onboarding of appropriately qualified employees in accordance with University policies and procedures. Work to resolve conflicts and grievances promptly, in accordance with University policies and procedures.   Citizenship and Service: Actively develop partnerships and strengthen relationships with colleagues, industry, government departments, professional bodies and the wider community. Demonstrate leadership of self and others through mentoring and collaboration. Efficiently manage allocated internal service roles and processes, including participation in decision-making and service on relevant committees. Effectively perform a range of administrative functions as required. Provide support to other academic staff as needed, including during absences. Consistently demonstrate the UQ values.   In addition to the above, responsibilities at Academic Level C (Senior Lecturer) will include:   Teaching: Build and maintain internal and external education collaborations, and undertake and initiate professional learning in teaching. Lead programs, disciplines, plans, courses and/or student cohorts.   Research: Contribute to the development or delivery of partnerships with research end users and external collaborations that produce high-quality research outputs. Successfully build teams and contribute to discipline service, including service on editorial boards.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Establish a track record of successful supervision outcomes. Demonstrate responsible research conduct and lead others in this practice. Effectively develop supervisees’ capabilities and skills, and manage researcher development. Demonstrate leadership in facilitating engagement opportunities for supervisees.   Citizenship and Service: Consolidate an established record of internal and external service and make appropriate leadership contributions.   This is a Teaching & Research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance and our Appointment Information Booklet .   About You  For Appointment at Level B (Lecturer) :   Applicants should hold a PhD in Economics or a directly relevant field by the time of commencement. You will also demonstrate: A growing profile in teaching and research in the discipline. An active research program and a pipeline of work in progress expected to lead to publications in world-leading and internationally excellent journals in Economics. The ability to deliver high-quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels across a range of settings, including small and large groups. The ability to coordinate courses and contribute to the development and ongoing improvement of programs, curricula and course materials in the discipline. The ability to supervise honours and higher degree by research students through to successful completion. Experience in seeking external research funding, or a clear intention to seek, secure and manage such funding. The ability to contribute to internal and external service activities.       For Appointment at Level C (Senior Lecturer) :   Applicants should hold a PhD in Economics or a directly relevant field. You will also demonstrate:   An established profile in teaching and research in the discipline. An active research program, a significant track record of publications in world-leading and internationally excellent journals, and a pipeline of work in progress targeting these journals in Economics. A strong record of securing and managing external research funding. A demonstrated record of high-quality teaching at undergraduate, honours and postgraduate levels across a range of settings, including small and large groups. A strong track record of course coordination and contributions to the development and ongoing improvement of programs, curricula and course materials in the discipline. A solid record of supervising honours and higher degree by research students through to successful completion. A growing record of meaningful contributions to internal service, alongside a strong contribution to external service activities.   About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.    The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.    Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Claudio Mezzetti via email c.mezzetti@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please upload your resume, cover letter, responses to the ‘About You’ criteria, a job market/research paper, and two written references or letters of recommendation . Please also ensure you answer all required application questions. Please apply through our listing on the AEA JOE Network : HERE . Please note that applications submitted via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 1st November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69343). Please note that interviews and seminars are expected to take place over November and December 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Manager--International-Development_R-68746-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, International Development",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nUQ International Development (UQID), Global Partnerships Full-time (100%), fixed term position through to October 2029. Base salary will be in the range $140,766 - $148,862 + 17% super (HEW Level 9). Based at our picturesque and conveniently located St Lucia campus (occasional domestic and international travel will be required) About This Opportunity UQ International Development (UQID) is one of the leading university development groups in the Asia-Pacific region, helping build the capacity of people and organisations around the world to achieve their development goals. Leveraging the knowledge, expertise and leadership of UQ staff and researchers, UQID delivers impactful international development initiatives, customised training programs, advisory services and technical assistance. This senior leadership opportunity will see you lead a high performing and collaborative project delivery function, working alongside diverse experts, partners and stakeholders to create meaningful change through internationally funded programs and partnerships. Drawing on UQ’s vision of creating change together, you will play a pivotal role in ensuring projects are delivered with excellence, innovation and lasting impact. Key responsibilities will include: Providing strategic leadership of UQID’s project delivery portfolio, ensuring alignment with organisational priorities and development objectives. Leading complex international development projects and programs, including project governance, financial oversight, contract management, monitoring and evaluation, and stakeholder engagement. Building and maintaining strong relationships with government agencies, donors, managing contractors, UQ faculties, researchers and international partners. Driving continuous improvement through the development of scalable systems, policies and processes that enhance project delivery outcomes. Leading, mentoring and supporting multidisciplinary project delivery teams to achieve excellence, performance targets and meaningful development impact. Contributing to business planning, tender development, contract negotiations, forecasting and strategic decision making across UQID, including engaging with internal and external partners to identify and scope opportunities for international development activities and contributing to the design and development of long term projects and programs. About You You are a strategic and collaborative leader with a passion for international development and a track record of delivering complex programs that create meaningful impact. You bring strong commercial and stakeholder management capability, thrive in dynamic and culturally diverse environments, and are motivated by the opportunity to work alongside experts, partners and communities to achieve sustainable development outcomes. We welcome candidates from international development, higher education, government, consulting, donor funded program delivery and related sectors who can demonstrate transferable leadership experience and a commitment to excellence. Essential Degree/qualifications in Development Practice, Public Policy, International Relations, Business, Project Management, Government, or another relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Significant experience leading complex project delivery, program management or international development initiatives, ideally within donor funded, government or international capacity building environments. Demonstrated success building and maintaining productive relationships with diverse stakeholders, including government agencies, donors, industry partners and senior leaders. Strong people leadership experience with the ability to guide multidisciplinary teams, drive performance and foster a collaborative, inclusive and high performing culture. Proven financial, contract and risk management capability, coupled with sound judgement, negotiation skills and a solutions-focused approach. Excellent written, verbal and presentation skills, with the ability to communicate complex information clearly and influence outcomes across a broad range of audiences. Desirable Experience contributing to the design and/or implementation of projects funded through vertical funds, such as the Global Environment Facility (GEF), Green Climate Fund (GCF), Adaptation Fund or similar multilateral financing mechanisms. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Melissa Tipping, Head, International Development, at m.tipping@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Applications close Monday 19 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68746). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 28 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Floor-Manager--AIBN_R-69214": {
      "title": "Floor Manager, AIBN",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Full-time (100%), permanent position with a dedicated and collaborative team Base salary will be in the range $106,709.01 - $116,198.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 7) Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  We are seeking a motivated and experienced Floor Manager to manage all aspects of the research floor operation and/or shared laboratory/research facilities and research equipment.   Reporting to the AIBN Infrastructure Manager, the Floor Manager will be responsible for all aspects of managing the shared facilities and research equipment on the floor as well as maintaining shared equipment. In this role, you will work closely with the other AIBN Floor Managers to coordinate activities and support throughout AIBN’s research facilities. In addition, you will work under the advisement and guidance provided by the AIBN’s Health Safety Wellness Manager.   Key responsibilities will include:    Floor Management Provide oversight and management of the laboratory and office areas. Manage the use of office space and desk allocation for the floor Interact with the various facilities staff from the individual Research Groups and the Facilities and other Floor Managers to coordinate activities Coordinates and manage the process for building repairs / alterations and requests, including coordinating the installation of building services such as power points, gas outlets Coordinates and manages infrastructure, assets, research equipment, communal work areas and inventory, including conducting stocktakes, audits, procurement, coordinating repair/works and managing other relevant processes Acts as Floor Warden (floor or building), Laser Safety Officer, Workplace Health and Safety Coordinator (WHSC) and/or First Aid Officer and advises/trains staff on HSW and other legislated responsibilities Participates in the local workplace health and safety committee Conduct workplace audits and risk assessments to identify hazards and unsafe workplace conditions and work practices and determines and monitors the implementation of appropriate corrective/control measures Provide inductions, training on specialised equipment/chemical handling and other relevant training for staff, visitors and contractors Train and supports research staff with documentation for OGTR, Ethics, DAFF Maintain records of AIBN equipment, building occupants and chemicals   Laboratory Management Management of central AIBN laboratory equipment, including scheduling, supply of consumables, ensuring correct use of instrumentation, providing training and servicing. Act as liaison between the research staff and the Institute’s Infrastructure Manager and the HSW Manager, including providing after-hours contact and support. Monitor the use of laboratory space and provide advice to the Infrastructure Manager on space utilisation. Facilitate the implementation of testing regimes RCD, Biosafety cabinets, fume cupboards etc. Coordinates the use of common areas and resources (eg. laboratory wash-up area and laboratory supplies) Coordinate infrastructure requirements to accommodate the installation of complex scientific equipment Coordinates of the research support activities on each floor and ensuring their smooth running with streamlined efficiency   About You   You will have: Bachelor’s degree (Honours) in Chemistry, Biology, Biochemical Engineering, or a related field, OR an equivalent combination of relevant education, training, and experience Broad scientific and technical knowledge, including specialist knowledge in Molecular Biology, Chemistry, or Biochemical Engineering Experience in a research environment with a minimum of 2 years in a similar role Experience in staff supervision, as well as developing and implementing policies and procedures Strong understanding of mechanical, electrical, and electronic equipment, with a methodical approach to problem-solving Effective written and oral communication skills, with the ability to work efficiently and organise tasks under pressure with excellent attention to detail Demonstrated ability to liaise with diverse stakeholders, including research staff and students, and contribute to laboratory operations Strong capacity for forward planning, task prioritisation, and meeting deadlines Ability to interpret and apply relevant legislation in work situations Workplace Health and Safety Coordinator Certification, and First Aid skills and HSW experience will be highly regarded Knowledge of relevant HSW, OGTR and DAFF Biosecurity legislative requirements and university operating procedures, and Specific equipment training will also be highly regarded eg. Autoclaves, flow cytometers     About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Luke Matthew - l.matthew2@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 13 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69214)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Crop-Quantitative-Genetics_R-68155-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Crop Quantitative Genetics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation. Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 + 17% super (Academic Level A). Based at our St Lucia Campus .   About This Opportunity Join a visionary and collaborative research community as a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Crop Quantitative Genetics. Working within UQ’s Queensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation, you will develop and apply advanced quantitative genetics methods to improve genomic prediction of complex traits in soybean. As part of the UQ-led ARC Training Centre in Predictive Breeding for Agricultural Futures, you will work with BASF and other research partners to explore Genotype-by-Environment-by-Management interactions and help refine innovative quantitative genetics methodologies for predictive breeding.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Research: Conduct univariate and multivariate statistical analyses of large, multidimensional crop datasets incorporating trait phenotypes, environmental descriptors and genomic information to investigate Genotype-by-Environment-by-Management interactions. Develop and apply mixed-model statistical methodology, including genomic prediction, crop simulation and machine learning, to improve prediction of crop yield and related traits. Supervision and Researcher Development: Assist with mentoring students, publish scholarly research and contribute to the development of collaborative relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through purposeful collaboration, contribute to service and administrative activities, support colleagues and participate actively in priority activities across QAAFI and the ARC Training Centre.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in Quantitative Genetics, Plant Breeding, Crop Genomics or a closely related field. An emerging research profile in quantitative genetics, predictive breeding, plant breeding or crop genomics. Demonstrated capability in the statistical analysis of large, multidimensional agricultural, genomic or environmental datasets. Experience with mixed models, genomic prediction, crop simulation, machine learning, hierarchical modelling or related quantitative methodologies. Evidence of publishing research in reputable refereed journals and communicating findings at conferences or other scholarly forums. Evidence of contributing to external research funding applications or other mechanisms that support collaborative research. The ability to work inclusively and effectively with researchers, students, industry partners and other research end users. A developing record of internal service, external engagement or contributions to collaborative academic and research activities.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   UQ researchers are creating purposeful change across agriculture by combining crop science, genetics and collaboration with growers. In 2025, UQ announced the development of five new pigeonpea varieties to support the wider adoption of this legume in Australia. Read more about UQ’s pioneering legume varieties for Australian growers .   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Melanie Wilkinson at m.wilkinson2@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of qualifications and the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State .   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 28 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-68155)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Manager--Infrastructure-Planning-and-Development--Rural-Health-_R-69044-1": {
      "title": "Manager, Infrastructure Planning and Development (Rural Health)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Health, Medicine and Behavioural Sciences Full-time (100%), continuing position Base salary will be in the range $120,317 to $134,892 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our Herston location , with travel to rural and regional clinical sites across Queensland About This Opportunity The Manager, Infrastructure Planning and Development (Rural) will coordinate infrastructure planning, development and optimisation across the Faculty’s rural and regional education and training network. Through complex stakeholder engagement with internal teams, health service partners, government agencies, community organisations and technical specialists, you will deliver fit-for-purpose infrastructure outcomes with tangible impacts on rural workforce development and healthcare. Your work will support evolving educational, clinical, research and accommodation needs while advancing the sustainable delivery of education and training throughout Queensland. Key responsibilities will include: Develop coordinated plans for the management, optimisation and future development of rural and regional infrastructure, including teaching, training, administrative and accommodation facilities. Lead investigations, needs analyses, feasibility assessments and business cases that inform evidence-based infrastructure decisions and future investment. Coordinate infrastructure projects from concept and planning through implementation, commissioning and review, monitoring scope, risk, timelines, budgets and outcomes. Build trusted partnerships and support effective governance, consultation, contracts, leases, licences and shared-facility arrangements across complex stakeholder environments. About You Relevant degree qualifications in project management, property, infrastructure, facilities management, business or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. Demonstrated experience in infrastructure planning, project development, facilities planning, property management or a related field within a large and complex organisation. Proven ability to coordinate infrastructure projects and planning initiatives involving multiple stakeholders, service providers and specialist consultants. Strong analytical and strategic thinking skills, including the ability to interpret complex information, assess emerging issues and provide evidence-based recommendations. Demonstrated capability in data analysis and reporting to support space utilisation, asset management, investment prioritisation and strategic decision-making. Experience using project management, asset management, property or business information systems to manage information, monitor progress and track outcomes. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with architects, engineers, project managers, contractors and other technical specialists, and communicate technical information clearly to non-technical stakeholders. Exceptional stakeholder engagement and written communication skills, with experience preparing business cases, strategic plans, reports, executive briefings and stakeholder communications. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Greg Hafner at g.hafner@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69044)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Student-and-Academic-Administration-Officer---Mechanical-and-Mining-Engineering_R-69639-1": {
      "title": "Student and Academic Administration Officer - Mechanical and Mining Engineering",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\n·         School of Mechanical and Mining Engineering – Faculty of EAIT ·         Full-time (100%), Permanent position ·         Base salary will be in the range $85,451.97 - $94,983.84 + 17% super (HEW Level 5) ·         Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  We are seeking a motivated and efficient Student and Academic Administration Officer to join our team. You will play a key role in supporting the smooth operation of the administrative functions of the School.    Key responsibilities will include:   Provide high-level administrative support to students, academic staff and professional colleagues within the School. Deliver timely and accurate advice in line with University policies, procedures and legislation. Act as the first point of contact for enquiries, resolving routine and complex matters. Process student administration including applications, extensions, deferred exams, enrolment and remarks. Support academic administration (course profiles, Blackboard updates, exams and grade processing). Coordinate meetings, projects and events, while maintaining systems, procedures and continuous improvement initiatives.   About You   An undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training.   Demonstrated experience providing high-quality administrative and customer service support in a high-volume environment.   High level of attention to detail and accuracy in written work, as well as a high level of organisational, time management, and problem-solving skills.  Proven ability to manage competing priorities, exercise sound judgement, and work effectively both independently and collaboratively.   Strong written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills, including the ability to communicate effectively with staff and students from diverse cultural backgrounds.   Demonstrated proficiency in the Microsoft Office suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook).   Strong working knowledge of student and academic administration processes, as well as relevant University policies and procedures, or the capacity to rapidly acquire this knowledge.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Miss Jacki Drinnen via j.drinnen@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks, and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday 14 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69639)."
