    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Associate-Lecturer--Anthropology-_R-69259-1": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer (Anthropology)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-30\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nSchool of Social Science Part-time (50%), fixed term position, up until 25 February 2027 Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% Superannuation (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia campus   About This Opportunity  The School of Social Science at The University of Queensland is seeking a part-time (0.5 FTE), fixed-term Associate Lecturer (Teaching Focused) to contribute to its undergraduate teaching program from 2 November 2026 to 25 February 2027.   In this role, you will deliver ANTH1030: Anthropology of Current World Issues during the Summer Semester.   The successful applicant will have an emerging profile of teaching practice through innovative teaching activities that advance student learning and be willing to engage, as a UQ citizen, in the collegial life of the School.   As this role is for a short, fixed-term contract and is based in Brisbane, we are not accepting applications from interstate or international candidates at this time.   Key responsibilities will include:  Teaching: Coordinate courses, develop and deliver lectures and tutorials, assess and mark students, maintain quality of courses, mentor students, provide academic counselling, and adhere to university teaching rules.   Citizenship and Service: Engage with the collegial life and activities of the School, including thesis marking where relevant and School ad hoc working parties, perform administrative functions, and uphold University values.   This is a teaching focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  Applicants should possess a PhD or near completion of a PhD in Anthropology. Additionally, you will demonstrate: Demonstrated knowledge in a relevant field or sub-field of Anthropology. Demonstrated ability to teach effective courses at the undergraduate and postgraduate levels using active learning and flexible approaches to teaching, including teaching online. Well-developed communication, interpersonal and consultative skills, and the ability to work collaboratively with colleagues in teaching and learning. Capacity to use a range of online systems and platforms in teaching and learning (e.g. Blackboard, electronic course profiles etc.). About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Suzanna Fay , Interim Head of School at s.fay@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Tuesday 13 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69259)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Officer---Health-Professions-Education_R-68105-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer - Health Professions Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nMedical School Part-time (0.4FTE), fixed-term position for up to 18 months   Base salary will be in the range $85,372 - $113,659 + 17% super (Academic Level A)  Based at our  Herston Campus   About This Opportunity    Are you passionate about shaping the future of healthcare education and workplace learning? Join UQ Medical School as a Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer and play a pivotal role in the nationally significant GEMPASS-funded project, Practice-Ready Medical Students: Ensuring Readiness for Team-based Collaborative Practice using a Participatory Approach . Working within a collaborative and inclusive research environment, you will partner with leading researchers, clinicians and educators from The University of Queensland, the University of Melbourne and Griffith University to generate new insights that strengthen how future doctors learn, collaborate and contribute within healthcare teams. This is an outstanding opportunity for an emerging researcher to influence medical education nationally while developing expertise in qualitative research, interprofessional learning and healthcare workforce development within a globally recognised university. Key responsibilities will include:    Research:  Conduct qualitative and participatory research activities, including participant recruitment and retention, interviews, longitudinal data collection, workplace-based research, ethics and governance processes, research data management, qualitative analysis, and the interpretation of findings to support project outcomes. Research Communication and Knowledge Translation:  Prepare research reports, scholarly publications, conference presentations and dissemination materials, while supporting the development of educational resources and knowledge translation initiatives that enhance medical students’ readiness for collaborative, team-based practice. Collaboration and Stakeholder Engagement:  Work closely with investigators, clinicians, educators, students and healthcare professionals across multiple universities and clinical settings, fostering productive partnerships and contributing to the successful delivery of project milestones and research outcomes. Citizenship and Service:  Contribute to the research culture of the UQ Medical School through active participation in project meetings, seminars and collaborative activities, while demonstrating behaviours aligned with UQ’s values and maintaining respectful and professional relationships with project partners and stakeholders. This is a  research focused  position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .    About You    Completion or near completion of a PhD in health professions education, education, social sciences, psychology, healthcare, or a related discipline, with a demonstrated commitment to research excellence. Experience undertaking qualitative research, including participant recruitment, interviews, longitudinal data collection, research data management, and qualitative analysis. Demonstrated interest in medical education, workplace learning, interprofessional education, healthcare communication, or related areas that support collaborative healthcare practice. Evidence of contributing to research outputs such as peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, reports, or other scholarly dissemination activities. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage effectively with students, clinicians, educators, healthcare professionals, and research partners across multiple organisations. Proven organisational and project management skills, with the ability to work both independently and collaboratively, manage competing priorities, and meet project deadlines in a dynamic research environment.   About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.     The greater  benefits  of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a  Group of Eight  university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.     Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Christy Noble, Associate Professor, Medical Education (Assessment of Clinical Skills) at c.noble2@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume ,  cover letter , and  responses to the ‘About You’ section.        Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.    Applications close Thursday 15 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68105)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Consultant---Biosafety_R-68937": {
      "title": "Principal Consultant – Biosafety",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nHealth, Safety and Wellness Full-time, fixed-term position through to December 2028 $106,709 to $116,199 + 17% super (HEW Level 7) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity Help enable world-class research by making complex biosafety requirements practical, proportionate and workable. UQ is home to a diverse range of teaching and research involving biological materials, specialist laboratories and research facilities. Behind that work is a Biosafety Advisory team helping our researchers navigate biological risks safely, confidently and in line with regulatory requirements. As a Principal Consultant, Biosafety , you’ll become a trusted specialist adviser across a portfolio of laboratories, facilities and research activities, working directly with researchers, supervisors and managers to find practical pathways for safe and compliant research. This is much more than checking boxes. You’ll apply your scientific expertise and judgement to complex biological risk assessments, laboratory inspections, incident reviews, containment requirements and genetically modified organism dealings , translating technical and regulatory requirements into controls that work in real research environments. You’ll also join the team at an important time, creating capacity to modernise biosafety systems, strengthen guidance and assurance tools, improve training and stakeholder communications, and support implementation of the R3 Lab Activity Module. What will you get involved in? ·         Specialist biosafety advice: Partner with researchers, supervisors and managers to provide practical advice across a diverse portfolio of laboratories, facilities and research activities. ·         Risk and assurance: Lead biological risk assessments, facility inspections, incident reviews and corrective action monitoring, including assessment of low-risk genetically modified organism dealings and hazardous biological applications. ·         Turning regulation into practice: Interpret biological safety procedures, containment standards and regulatory requirements and translate them into proportionate controls for infectious agents, GMOs, biological samples and related materials. ·         Committee and governance support: Contribute specialist expertise to biosafety committee processes and support consistent, well-informed decision-making. ·         Continuous improvement: Help improve procedures, guidance, training materials, assurance tools and communications, while supporting the modernisation of UQ’s biosafety systems.   If you enjoy the point where science, risk and problem-solving meet , this is an opportunity to use your expertise to help researchers do ambitious work safely, rather than simply telling them what they can’t do. About You You’re a biosafety or biological sciences professional who can combine strong technical knowledge with sound judgement and a practical approach. You’ll be comfortable stepping into a laboratory, understanding the science and the risk, asking the right questions and working collaboratively with researchers to find an appropriate way forward. You’ll bring: ·         A degree in biological sciences, microbiology, biotechnology or a related field, together with substantial relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience. ·         Specialist knowledge of biological safety, containment, biological risk assessment and laboratory compliance. ·         Experience leading inspections, risk assessments, compliance reviews or incident investigations in biological or laboratory environments. ·         The ability to interpret policies, procedures, containment standards and regulatory requirements and apply them practically within research and operational environments. ·         Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, including the ability to identify systemic issues and develop practical, proportionate solutions. ·         High-level communication, consultation and influencing skills, with the ability to build credibility with both technical and non-technical stakeholders. ·         The ability to work independently, manage competing priorities and contribute effectively within a collaborative specialist team. ·         A genuine commitment to continuous improvement and supporting safe research practice. ·         Experience in a university or research institute, laboratory or facility management, biological compliance, Microsoft 365 or database systems will be highly regarded.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Kerrin Henderson at k.henderson@uq.edu.au . For application enquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , quoting R-68937 in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. This role involves entering laboratories and animal facilities where personal protective equipment must be worn and where exposure to animal allergens may occur. Immunisation against certain vaccine-preventable diseases, including hepatitis B where relevant to the work undertaken, may be required. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68937)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Long-Pocket/Senior-Editor_R-69155-1": {
      "title": "Senior Editor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nUniversity of Queensland Press Full-time (100%), permanent position Base salary will be in the range $97,268 - $104,348 + 17% super (HEW Level 6)  Based at our  Long Pocket location      About This Opportunity  Join University of Queensland Press (UQP), one of Australia’s most respected publishing houses as a Senior Editor and help bring exceptional stories and ideas to life. Reporting to the Managing Editor and working closely with a collaborative, inclusive and purposeful publishing team, the Senior Editor will independently manage the editorial and production lifecycle of a diverse portfolio of adult fiction, non-fiction and children’s publishing titles. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a culture of excellence while working with authors, creatives and publishing professionals to create meaningful impact through books that inform, inspire and connect communities. The role reflects UQ’s commitment to creating change together through collaboration, innovation and high-quality outcomes.   Key responsibilities will include, but are not limited to:  Independently project manage the editorial and production process for multiple book titles from manuscript to print publication. Undertake complex structural editing, copy editing and manuscript assessment to maintain the highest editorial standards. Build and maintain strong professional relationships with authors, freelancers, designers, proofreaders, typesetters and other publishing partners. Write compelling cover copy and marketing content and collaborate with sales and marketing teams on title positioning and promotion.   About You   Tertiary degree in English, Journalism, Communication or a Diploma in Editing and Publishing. Minimum of three years’ experience working within a trade publishing house or another relevant editorial position. Experience in evaluating and editing the content, structure and format of a range of written material. Strong project management skills, from first author contact through to print. Advanced verbal and written communication skills with a demonstrated ability to develop strong professional relationships with authors and liaise with suppliers, including other editors, designers and proofreaders. Ability to work effectively under pressure to tight deadlines and within budget. Exceptional time management and organisational skills. Demonstrated ability to project-manage multiple, competing tasks. Excellent computer skills and experience working with Microsoft Office Suite, Adobe Acrobat and InDesign.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, free parking at Long Pocket and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Jacqueline Blanchard at jaqueline.blanchard@uqp.com.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69155)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---UQ-Business-School_R-69862": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - UQ Business School",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nCasual\n\nThe University of Queensland Business School   The University of Queensland Business School is independently ranked as one of the top business schools in Australia and among the leading institutions worldwide. Our mission is to cultivate courageous thinkers who empower future leaders to positively transform business and society.    Based at the St Lucia campus as part of the Faculty of Business, Economics and Law, the School brings together 12,000 students and 200 subject experts.     About This Expression of Interest   The University of Queensland Business School is seeking expressions of interest from Tutors and Facilitators to be engaged on a casual basis.    Casual Academics at The University of Queensland Business School play a significant part in the overall student experience. As such, our successful candidates will participate in the education of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students within the key discipline areas of Accounting, Finance, Business Information Systems, Management, Strategy & Entrepreneurship, International Business, Marketing and Tourism within the School by preparing and delivering tutorials, marking assessment items, conducting student consultations and fostering a positive learning environment. Facilitators are also being sought to assist in activity based seminars, tutorials and workshops.    These positions are located at our picturesque St Lucia campus , renowned as one of Australia’s most attractive university campuses, and located just 7km from Brisbane’s city centre. Bounded by the Brisbane River on three sides, and with outstanding public transport connections, our 114-hectare site provides a perfect work environment – you can enjoy the best of both worlds: a vibrant campus with the tradition of an established university.    Our Ideal Candidate   As a Casual Academic, you'll have a minimum of an undergraduate degree, with a minimum GPA of 5.0, enrolment in a relevant postgraduate program (coursework or HDR), and/or relevant Industry experience ideally within a business or related discipline. Your skills in teaching will be demonstrable and effective, with teaching evaluations (or other evidence) to support your claims. You'll be proficient in using eLearning systems, such as Blackboard, or are capable of swiftly learning new software. Experience in public speaking and leading small groups will be highly regarded, plus a proven ability to work autonomously and meet deadlines.    You must have ongoing unrestricted work rights in Australia to apply for this opportunity.     We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. Our Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au .    What We Can Offer   This is a Casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. Casual Academics at The University of Queensland Business School are paid in accordance with The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement).    For further information on casual rates, please visit our casual academic salaries page .     We invest in the development of our Casual Academics through training sessions, plus a host of other University wide development initiatives.    Questions?   If you have any questions regarding this expression of interest, please contact The University of Queensland Business School's Casual Support team at casualsupport@business.uq.edu.au .    Want to Submit an Expression of Interest?   Please hit the APPLY button and follow the prompts.    Expressions of interest must be submitted by Tuesday 27th October 2026 at 11:00pm (AEST) for consideration for semesters 1 & 2 in 2027. If you submit an expression of interest after this date, you may be considered for vacancies that arise throughout the year or the year following.     If you are a current UQ Employee, unpaid staff member or scholar please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity via our Internal Job Board.      To satisfy pre-requisite questions and ensure your application can be considered in full, all candidates must apply via the UQ Workday portal . Applications received via other channels including direct email will not be accepted.      Applications close: Tuesday 27th October 2026"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---UQ-School-of-Economics_R-69707": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - UQ School of Economics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted Today\nCasual\n\nSchool of Economics    Casual Position    Applications Close on 21 October 2026 at 11:00PM AEST     About this opportunity     The successful appointees will undertake delivery of tutorials with associated preparation, student consultation and marking in first year, second year, third year and postgraduate economics courses.     This position is based at the St Lucia Campus and successful appointees must be available to attend on campus.   Casual Academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) a nd reiterated in this job profile.        Delivery or presentation of tutorials Tutorial means any educational delivery described as a tutorial in a course or unit outline, or in an official timetable issued by the employer. Casual Academics in the School of Economics are required to deliver or present a tutorial (or equivalent delivery through other than face-to-face teaching mode) of a specified duration and relatedly provide non-contact duties in the nature of preparation, directly-associated and contemporaneous marking (see Enterprise Agreement), and student consultation.  These non-contact duties will be directly associated with, and arising from, the delivery of a specific tutorial.     Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):   Preparing and delivering theoretical and/or practical tutorials which are engaging, context-relevant and where possible, participative.    Tutoring students in face to face delivery mode.   Providing support to students, including, consultation, and assessment feedback.  Undertaking marking of assessment tasks, as directed by course coordinators, with hours for marking to be confirmed by School Administration staff prior to commencing marking. Maintaining, and where possible improving, the quality of tutorials, as measured through evaluation instruments to meet industry and educational standards.  Providing tutorial support for other positions during planned or unexpected absences.  Understanding and applying University Rules relevant to teaching and learning practice.  Maintaining class participation and attendance lists as required.  Maintaining a high level of performance on evaluations by students.  Attending meetings, induction sessions or required training sessions.    Compliance     All Casual academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:  The University’s Code of Conduct .  Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.  The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .  Requirements of the Education Services  for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.     About the School of Economics     UQ’s School of Economics is a leading provider of economics education in Australia and ranks in the top 50 among Economics departments worldwide for its research.    Located on the St Lucia campus as part of the Faculty of Business, Economics and Law, the School is home to more than 2753 domestic and international students, 17 professional staff and 66 active teaching and research staff. Its accomplished scholars claim a large share of Australia’s economics honours, national teaching awards, competitive research grants, fellowships, publications and media coverage. The School also has an established research centre and an active seminar and visitor program, providing exposure to researchers from around the world.      About You     Candidates enrolled in the BECON or BAFE must have completed at least 10 units (5 courses) of UQ ECON-coded courses by July 2026 and achieved a GPA of 6.5 or better in economics courses. Candidates enrolled in the BPPE, the Bachelor of Arts (Economics Major), the Bachelor of Business Management (Business Economics Major), Bachelor of Commerce (Finance Major), or the Bachelor of Advanced Business (Finance Major) must have completed at least 6 units (3 courses) of UQ ECON-coded courses by July 2026 and achieved a GPA of 6.5 or better in economics courses. Candidates enrolled in the Economics Masters program must have completed at least one semester full time of postgraduate study including 8 units (4 courses) of ECON-coded courses and achieved a GPA of 6.5 or better.     UQ PhD in Economics candidates are encouraged to apply and do not need to have studied previously at UQ to be eligible.   We welcome and encourage applications from female candidates.  What we can offer     This is a Casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate is $57.60 per hour, plus super.  For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit  Why Work at UQ  and review Operational UQ Enterprise Agreement_2021-2026.pdf .      Questions?     To discuss this role please contact Marie Taylor at m.taylor16@uq.edu.au .        For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number in the subject line.      Want to apply?     All applicants must provide the following documents:    Resume  Most recent academic study report    To ensure your application is considered, you must apply via the UQ Careers portal by the job closing deadline. Applications received via other channels, including direct email, will not be accepted."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Legal-Counsel_R-69640-1": {
      "title": "Legal Counsel",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nLegal Services Division , based within the Office of the Chief Operating Officer Full-time (100%), permanent position in a high performing in-house legal team Strong support for your professional development and career growth Base salary will be in the range $120,317.28 - $134,892.52 + 17% super (HEW Level 8) Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  As Legal Counsel, you will be responsible for providing legal advice on a broad range of property and commercial matters including commercial property transactions, strategic projects and commercial contracts.  The position also provides legal advice on a wide range of other legal issues affecting UQ.   Working in an in-house environment, this unique career opportunity will allow you to be flexible and available to work across all streams of legal work (depending on client demands, expertise and priorities as directed by the Directors and General Counsel). You will undertake work ranging in complexity and will work with and under the direction of the Directors and other senior lawyers in Legal Services as well as assisting the General Counsel in managing the interests of UQ.   You will join a busy, supportive and collaborative in-house legal team that values professional excellence, sound judgement and frank and fearless advice. Your contribution will help protect UQ’s interests, strengthen decision-making and deliver meaningful outcomes across the University.   Key responsibilities will include but are not limited to:  Providing high-quality legal advice to senior leaders and key decision-makers across UQ on property, commercial, contractual and other legal matters. Drafting, negotiating and advising on commercial agreements and property documents, including sale and purchase contracts, leases, licences, easements, construction and development agreements, tender documents and collaboration agreements. Advising on statutory interpretation, governance, administrative law, privacy, confidentiality, financial accountability, policy development and compliance with legal obligations. Building trusted stakeholder relationships and contributing to continuous improvement, legal capability, precedent development and the effective management of current and emerging legal risks. A full position description is available on request by contacting talent@uq.edu.au   About You   You must be admitted as a Solicitor or Barrister of the Supreme Court of Queensland and have demonstrated relevant expertise and experience, with a minimum of four years’ post-admission experience preferred. You hold, or are eligible to hold upon appointment, a Queensland practising certificate. You have extensive experience in property and commercial law, including advising on property transactions, infrastructure or development projects, strategic procurement and complex commercial matters. You demonstrate strong legal drafting skills and the ability to analyse and resolve complex issues with sound judgement, commercial acumen and meticulous attention to detail. You communicate with influence and possess strong written, interpersonal and negotiation skills, enabling you to work effectively with a diverse range of clients and stakeholders. You can manage multiple matters, adjust priorities and deliver accurate, timely advice in a busy environment with changing demands. You build productive working relationships and can work independently while contributing positively to a collaborative, inclusive and service-focused legal team. You demonstrate integrity, resilience and strategic awareness, with the confidence to provide impartial and forthright advice while recognising when guidance from senior lawyers is required. An understanding of the legislative framework applying to UQ, or experience acting for a statutory body or government entity, would be advantageous. Experience in information technology law would also be well regarded.   About Legal Services Led by the General Counsel, UQ Legal Services is responsible for the delivery of legal services to the Vice-Chancellor, executive management, and other key decision makers at UQ to support the achievement of UQ’s strategic objectives while protecting its interests. The General Counsel reports to the Chief Operating Officer and is part of the Chief Operating Officer’s senior management team.   UQ Legal Services has 6 major practice areas comprising: Commercial Advisory and Contracts; Property and Projects; Disputes and Regulatory Law; Workplace Relations; Trusts, Gifts and Work Integrated Learning; and Research and Intellectual Property.   The Legal Services team provides legal advice on a wide range of areas, including: commercial and contract law matters; property law matters; information technology matters; dispute resolution and litigation; administrative law, including advice to ensure defensible decisions by key decision-makers; student disciplinary and appeals matters; research misconduct investigations and processes; research related contracts and advices; governance issues, regulatory issues and statutory interpretation; bequests and gifts to UQ; intellectual property issues.   You can find more information here about Legal Services at UQ.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Leigh Weyer - l.weyer@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration (e.g. QLS + possession of QLD Practising Certificate), criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69640). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 26 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academic---UQ-School-of-the-Environment_R-69870": {
      "title": "Casual Academic - UQ School of the Environment",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nCasual\n\nCasual Academic - UQ School of the Environment Applications Close on 2nd November 2026 at 11:59PM AEST      About this opportunity   The successful appointees will support the Course Coordinator in the delivery of course aims and learning outcomes, in first year, second year, third year and postgraduate courses in the School of the Environment.   Casual academic positions are based at both St Lucia and Gatton Campuses depending on the course you are selected for. Successful appointees must be available to attend on campus.  Casual Academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) a nd reiterated in this job profile.      Responsibilities as a Casual Academic may include (but are not limited to):   Demonstrator support for the conduct of practical classes, demonstrations, student field work and excursions Marking Exam invigilation Preparation time Facilitation of a class activity not requiring the development of materials Consultation with students Attendance at induction sessions Undertaking of training as required Moderating discussion forums, being available for student consultation online or in person, being available for consultation/ assistance on a specific activity such as a simulation and where no preparation or follow-up is required outside of scheduled hours; and Attendance at, or involvement in, other academic activities as directed by their supervisor. Compliance   All Casual academics are required to comply with legislation and University policy relevant to the duties undertaken, including but not exclusive to:  The University’s Code of Conduct .  Requirements of the Queensland occupational health and safety (OH&S) legislation and related OH&S responsibilities and procedures developed by the University or Institute/School.  The adoption of sustainable practices in all work activities and compliance with associated legislation and related University sustainability responsibilities and procedures .  Requirements of the Education Services  for Overseas Students Act 2000, the National Code 2007 and associated legislation, and related responsibilities and procedures developed by the University.    About the School of the Environment Established in 2023, UQ’s  School of the Environment  brings together multidisciplinary researchers across the physical, biological and social sciences to address the UN Sustainable Development Goals—particularly climate action, life on land, and life below water—through globally relevant research, training and impact. The School makes a major contribution to UQ’s standing, ranking 1st in Australia and 7th globally for Environment/Ecology, and 1st in Australia and 13th worldwide for Environmental Science & Engineering (2025 NTU Performance Ranking of Scientific Papers for World Universities). These strengths support a research culture that connects world‑class capability with real‑world impact.  The  School’s research  spans environmental management; geography and geospatial science; earth and ocean science; biodiversity and conservation science, ecology, genetics and evolution; zoology; and marine biology. With more than 100 academic group leaders, over 100 postdoctoral fellows, UQ’s largest graduate student cohort, and more than $26M in external research funding annually, the School offers a highly collaborative environment for ambitious research and education.  Research within the School integrates fundamental and applied approaches to advance environmental knowledge and deliver practical solutions. This work is supported by specialist analytical and field capabilities in biomonitoring and environmental diagnostics, spatial and statistical analysis, sustainability assessment, and  geoanalytical and chemical methods . Strong partnerships with industry, government and community organisations further enhance research translation and impact. Researchers and students also benefit from three field‑based stations at  Heron Island ,  Moreton Bay  and  Hidden Vale .  The School delivers a comprehensive and interdisciplinary curriculum, emphasising theory, practice and professionalism, and serving more than 1,600 coursework students across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. Learning is enriched through contemporary digital platforms, high‑quality workshops and practical activities, and exceptional field experiences in Australia and overseas.  About You   We welcome expressions of interest from research assistants, postdoctoral fellows, honours, undergraduate/postgraduate and Higher Degree Research students, and applicants who have previously taught in their field at a significant level who want to return to the workforce.  What we can offer     This is a  casual position. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual hourly rate can be viewed  here .   Most casual academics rates will be within the AC30 and AC50 pay scales.    For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit  Why Work at UQ  and review  Operational UQ Enterprise Agreement_2021-2026.pdf .   Questions?     To discuss this role please contact SENV CAHP team: senv.cahp@uq.edu.au . For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference   Want to apply?      All applicants must provide the following documents:     Resume      To ensure your application is considered, you must apply via the UQ Careers portal by the job closing deadline. Applications received via other channels, including direct email, will not be accepted.     If you are a current employee of the University, including current casual staff and HDR scholars, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Director--Planning-and-Business-Intelligence_R-68685-1": {
      "title": "Director, Planning and Business Intelligence",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nLead a highly regarded Planning and Business Intelligence function at a globally recognised university Partner with the Vice-Chancellor, Provost, and senior leaders to shape the University's planning, performance, reporting, analytics and business intelligence Guide a high-performing multidisciplinary team and collaborate with key stakeholders across the University About the Opportunity Universities today operate in an increasingly complex and rapidly changing environment. Within this context, Planning and Business Intelligence (PBI) is a trusted resource for UQ's senior management and broader community, providing the management information and analysis that underpin evidence-based decision-making. PBI delivers five key functions: Strategic and operational planning Business intelligence Federal Government reporting Student load planning, admissions and program development Strategic analysis, surveys and dashboards This is a rare opportunity to influence how one of Australia's leading universities understands its performance, responds to a rapidly changing higher education landscape and makes strategic decisions about its future. Reporting to the Provost and working closely with the Vice-Chancellor and senior leaders, you will lead the University's planning, performance, reporting, analytics and business intelligence functions. You will provide high-quality information, analysis and advice on institutional performance, higher education policy and funding, student demand and growth, resource planning, and other critical matters informing UQ's strategic direction. Leading a high-performing, multidisciplinary team, you will work across the University to ensure leaders have the data and insights they need to plan effectively, respond to change and make well-informed decisions. About You We are seeking a strategic, collaborative and highly credible leader with relevant postgraduate qualifications and extensive experience in the Australian higher education sector. The ideal candidate will bring a strong understanding of government policy, funding methodologies and reporting requirements, together with demonstrated expertise in institutional planning, performance management, student load forecasting, admissions modelling and business intelligence. You will be an accomplished analytical thinker who can interpret complex quantitative and qualitative information, identify emerging trends and translate evidence into clear, practical advice that informs university-wide strategy, resourcing and performance. You will also be an experienced people leader who can guide and motivate a diverse multidisciplinary team, sustain a high-performing and responsive culture, and lead complex organisation-wide processes in a data-rich environment. Exceptional communication, influencing and relationship-building skills are essential, including the capacity to present complex analysis persuasively, advise senior decision-makers, negotiate strategic outcomes and represent the University effectively with government agencies, sector bodies and other key stakeholders. Learn More For a confidential discussion or to receive an Information Booklet, please contact: Andrew Barr , Practice Lead – Education, Slade Group Phone: 0400 762 660 Email: abarr@sladegroup.com.au How to Apply To apply, visit sladegroup.com.au and select Current Opportunities. Your application should include: A comprehensive résumé A cover letter A written response to the selection criteria (maximum two pages)   Applications close Friday the 23rd October, 11pm ( R-69525)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Manager--HR-and-Payroll-Operations_R-69868-1": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, HR and Payroll Operations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nHuman Resources Division Full-time, fixed-term position until 31 December 2027 Base salary will be in the range $140,766 - $148,862 + 17% super (HEW Level 9)  Based at our  St Lucia location      About This Opportunity  As the Senior Manager, Salary and Benefits , you will lead the strategic and operational delivery of the University's salary, superannuation, payroll, and employee benefits services. Playing a pivotal role within Human Resources, you will ensure our remuneration and benefits programs are compliant, efficient, and employee-focused, supporting a diverse workforce across a large and complex organisation. Leading a team of payroll, superannuation and benefits professionals, you will drive continuous improvement, enhance service delivery, and provide expert advice on remuneration, superannuation, salary packaging, taxation, and legislative compliance. This is an outstanding opportunity for an experienced leader to influence enterprise-wide practices while ensuring exceptional outcomes for staff and stakeholders. Duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Lead the delivery of payroll, superannuation, salary packaging, and employee benefits services, ensuring compliance with legislative, regulatory, audit, and governance requirements. Provide strategic and operational leadership in the management of complex payroll and remuneration matters, including employee benefits, taxation, salary packaging, overpayments, and payroll risk management. Act as the University's subject matter expert, delivering specialist advice on superannuation, payroll legislation, taxation requirements, and employee benefits programs. Drive business improvement initiatives to enhance operational efficiency, service quality, and stakeholder experience while ensuring compliance with UniSuper performance standards. Oversee financial reporting activities, including the preparation and distribution of monthly payroll and benefits-related reports. Lead reviews of business processes, systems, and policies to identify opportunities for innovation, continuous improvement, and risk mitigation. About UQ  As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.    About You You are an experienced people leader with deep expertise in payroll operations, employee benefits, and remuneration governance within a large and complex environment. You combine technical knowledge with a strategic mindset and are passionate about delivering exceptional service and continuous improvement. You will bring: Postgraduate qualifications in a relevant discipline and extensive leadership experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training, and significant professional expertise. Comprehensive knowledge of payroll, superannuation, salary packaging, taxation, and employment-related legislation. Demonstrated success leading complex payroll or remuneration functions within a large organisation. Strong understanding of risk management, governance, compliance, and audit requirements relating to payroll and employee benefits. Proven ability to influence, negotiate, and build trusted relationships with stakeholders at all levels. Exceptional leadership capabilities, with a track record of developing high-performing teams through coaching, mentoring, and professional development. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to navigate complex remuneration and payroll matters. A commitment to continuous improvement and delivering a responsive, customer-focused service experience.   Interested?  For more information about this opportunity, please contact Sandy Tacey, Director, People Services at   s.tacey@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.     Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69868). Applications will be reviewed as received and UQ reserves the right to close this recruitment process prior to the advertised close date."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Student-and-Academic-Administration-Officer---Identified--s105-_R-66125-2": {
      "title": "Student and Academic Administration Officer - Identified (s105)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-02\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology (EAIT) Full-time (100%), Permanent position Base salary will be in the range $85,451.97 - $94,983.84 + 17% super (HEW Level 5) Supporting the Student and Academic Administration Office, this position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only as an equal opportunity measure  Based at our St Lucia Campus   Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.    About This Opportunity  We are seeking a motivated Indigenous person to be a Student and Academic Administration Officer with excellent time management and communication skills. In this role you will play a vital role in delivering high-quality support to all students and academic staff within the Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology Faculty (EAIT). As a key member of the Student Administration team, you will provide professional advice, efficient service, and accurate information across a wide range of student lifecycle activities for domestic, international, undergraduate and postgraduate students – from commencement to graduation. This is a great opportunity if you wish to work in a very positive environment.   Key responsibilities will include:    Provide timely and accurate information to students, staff and stakeholders in line with University policies and legislation. Process student applications and documentation in the areas of: Academic progression, enrolment, credit assessment, graduation checking and exchange experiences. Prepare and deliver recommendations on student matters to support decision making. Support the review and continuous improvement of procedures and processes. Ensure the accuracy of information across University systems, websites and publications. Provide administrative and secretarial support for meetings, committees and working groups. Support the implementation and improvement of Student Administration workflows, processes and reporting. Represent the University at engagement activities and undertake other duties as required.   For further information, please click here to view the information booklet .   About You   The University of Queensland considers that the filling of this position is intended to constitute a special / equal opportunity measure under section 105 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld) and section 8(1) of the Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth).    The position is therefore only open to Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander applicants. The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.   Essential   A degree or diploma with relevant experience or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and training. A sound understanding of administrative processes relevant to student and academic administration, or the ability to rapidly gain such knowledge. Demonstrated ability to establish priorities and meet deadlines. High level of attention to detail and accuracy in written work. High level written, verbal and interpersonal communication skills. The ability to work collaboratively and effectively within the team and across other organisational units. A strong orientation to the provision of a high level of customer service An empathy with and ability to communicate with students, including those from diverse cultural backgrounds.   Desirable   Experience in a tertiary education institution or other large organisation in an area related to student administration would be highly regarded. Knowledge of University student computer systems and packages e.g. Microsoft office, SI-net, and experience with using CRM systems.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.   UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Amira Smith via amira.smith@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your skills and experience align to the ‘About You’ section.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close on Wednesday 14 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-66125)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Principal-Consultant--Work-Injury-Management_R-70087-1": {
      "title": "Senior Principal Consultant, Work Injury Management",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nHealth, Safety and Wellness Full-time, fixed-term appointment through to 31 January 2027, with the possibility of extension Base salary of $120,317 - $134,893 + 17% superannuation (HEW Level 8) Based at UQ’s St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced workers’ compensation professional to step into a senior role within one of Queensland’s largest and most complex organisations. Joining UQ’s Health, Safety and Wellness team, you will bring your expertise to a large self-insured workers’ compensation environment, managing complex physical and psychological injury claims and working closely with a broad range of internal and external stakeholders. This fixed-term appointment offers the chance to make an immediate impact, broaden your experience within a large self-insured organisation and work across matters that will genuinely draw on your technical expertise, judgement and stakeholder management capability. With the possibility of extension, you’ll have the opportunity to gain valuable experience within UQ while contributing to meaningful outcomes from day one. This opportunity may particularly suit an experienced claims or injury management professional looking to broaden their self-insurance experience, step into a senior-level role, or gain exposure to a large and complex organisational environment. As Senior Case Manager, Work Injury Management , you will have the autonomy to manage complex matters while also contributing your expertise across the broader team through strategic claim reviews, coaching, mentoring and continuous improvement initiatives. Key responsibilities will include: Manage a portfolio of complex physical and psychological injury workers’ compensation claims, from investigation and determination through to rehabilitation, return to work and claim resolution. Apply your technical expertise and professional judgement to complex matters involving rejection, cessation, review, appeal and common law claims. Coordinate medico-legal reports, submissions, reasons for decision and other complex claims documentation within UQ’s self-insured environment. Partner with injured workers, leaders, treating practitioners, medical specialists, rehabilitation providers, investigators and other stakeholders to achieve safe, sustainable and timely return-to-work outcomes. Provide specialist advice, coaching and mentoring to colleagues and stakeholders, including strategic review of complex and long-duration claims. Contribute to the ongoing improvement of Work Injury Management practices, policies and procedures, and provide leadership support across the team, including relieving the Work Injury Management Manager when required.     About You You will be an experienced workers’ compensation professional who is comfortable navigating complex and sensitive matters, exercising sound judgement and building trusted relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. You will bring: Undergraduate qualifications in a relevant field such as law, business, management or allied health, with at least five years of subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and/or education and training. Strong knowledge of workers’ compensation principles and legislation, with demonstrated capability interpreting and applying Queensland workers’ compensation and rehabilitation legislation. Proven experience managing complex statutory workers’ compensation claims and common law damages claims. Highly developed investigative, analytical and problem-solving skills, with the confidence to exercise professional judgement and discretion in complex matters. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to negotiate, advocate and communicate sensitively with a broad range of stakeholders. A collaborative approach and demonstrated ability to establish trusted relationships with injured workers, leaders, health professionals, colleagues and other stakeholders. Leadership capability, including experience providing constructive coaching, mentoring and technical guidance to others. Demonstrated experience preparing complex management reports within a workers’ compensation claims management environment. Experience within a self-insured organisation will be highly regarded.   About UQ At UQ, you will be part of a community of talented people working together to create positive change. In addition to joining a collaborative and experienced Health, Safety and Wellness team, you will have access to a range of benefits designed to support your career, wellbeing and life outside of work. These include 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking and salary packaging options. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Lucy Beikoff, Director, Health, Safety and Wellness at l.beikoff@uq.edu.au For application enquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , quoting the job reference number below. To apply, please upload your resume and a cover letter summarising how your background and experience align with the ‘About You’ section. Applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this position. We are committed to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ. We encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their capability and potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support accessibility requirements throughout the recruitment process. Please contact talent@uq.edu.au or +61 7 3365 2623 to discuss how we can assist. Applications close Sunday 25 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-70087)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Grants-Officer_R-67442-1": {
      "title": "Senior Grants Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nResearch Office 2 full time positions (one continuing, and one fixed term through to September 2027). Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Champion research excellence across diverse disciplines by leading high stakes grant rounds within a collaborative and supportive team environment. Move beyond delivery into leadership, where your judgement guides a specialist team and strengthens the bids UQ puts forward. Based at our Toowong High Street office. About This Opportunity The Research Office is seeking two Senior Grants Officers (Pre-Award) to help strengthen the quality, competitiveness and compliance of UQ’s national and international research funding bids. Working in an agile, collaborative and newly organised portfolio based team, you will partner with researchers, faculties, institutes and central teams to guide complex grant rounds from planning through to submission. This is a purposeful opportunity to step into senior advisory and leadership work, where your judgement, influence and eye for detail will help UQ researchers create change through research that matters. Key responsibilities will include: Lead end to end pre-award activities for complex national and international grant rounds, including planning timelines, coordinating inputs and managing competing priorities. Provide authoritative, practical and tailored advice on funding rules, eligibility, compliance and non-standard pre-award matters. Deliver high quality application reviews, guidance materials, workshops, presentations and briefings that build grant readiness across UQ. Mentor and support less experienced staff, contribute to a positive team culture, and identify opportunities to improve systems, workflows and service delivery. Additional requirements: Occasional travel to other UQ campuses. Additional working hours during peak periods. Leave restrictions during peak periods. About You You are a confident and collaborative professional who enjoys working in a fast paced environment where quality, stakeholder relationships and sound judgement matter. You bring a strong advisory mindset, can navigate complexity with confidence, and are motivated by helping researchers secure funding that delivers meaningful impact. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply complex funding rules, guidelines and policy frameworks. Experience coordinating competing priorities, managing deadlines and delivering high quality outcomes in a deadline driven environment. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to provide advice, deliver presentations and build trusted stakeholder relationships. Proven ability to exercise sound judgement and provide practical solutions on complex or non standard matters. Experience mentoring, coaching or leading others, and contributing to service improvement, process optimisation or operational excellence. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. In 2025, UQ News highlighted the continuing impact of research funding, including a UQ researcher receiving $450,000 from the Passe & Williams Foundation to improve health outcomes for patients with a rare form of cancer. Read more about UQ research grants . Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Josh at j.sweeney@research.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. If you wish to be considered for only one of the available positions (either the continuing or fixed term opportunity), please indicate this in your cover letter. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 21 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67442). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for 29 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Long-Pocket/Administration-Officer--UQ-Biological-Resources-_R-69230-1": {
      "title": "Administration Officer (UQ Biological Resources)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nUQ Biological Resources Full-time, fixed-term position up to 22 October 2027 Base salary will be in the range $85,451 - $94,983 + 17% super (HEW Level 5) Based at our Long Pocket campus location   About This Opportunity Join the UQ Biological Resources (UQBR) team in this dynamic Administration Officer role, where you’ll play an important part in supporting the smooth and efficient operation of one of the University’s key research infrastructure centres. Based at Long Pocket, you will contribute to the coordination of essential administrative and operational processes that enable world-class animal research at UQ. Working within a collaborative and supportive team environment, you will help streamline workflows, maintain critical animal management database systems, support continuous improvement initiatives and provide high-quality administrative services across UQBR operations. This is an excellent opportunity for a motivated and organised professional to contribute to a purpose-driven team that plays a vital role in advancing UQ’s research excellence. Key responsibilities will include: Maintain and update the Mosaic animal management database, support data integrity activities, assist with ethics protocol management, create, and review online training material and provide training and guidance to staff and students Support daily animal movements, orders, sales and deliveries across UQ campuses, ensuring efficient and accurate administration processes, when required Conduct asset stocktakes, maintain equipment and asset registers, coordinate equipment movements, and support office administration systems and workflows Provide specialised administrative support to the Director and Administration Manager, including stakeholder liaison, billing processes, reporting assistance, record management and general operational support About You Completion of a degree with limited subsequent relevant experience; or completion of an advanced diploma qualification with at least one year of subsequent relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training Demonstrated administration and coordination experience, including the ability to review work practices and contribute to improvements in procedures and processes Strong proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and experience maintaining databases, managing data integrity and supporting and training users Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to provide high-quality customer service and build effective working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines in a dynamic environment Demonstrated initiative, sound judgement and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively as part of a team An understanding of research involving animals and the importance of accurate data collection, compliance and record keeping Demonstrated understanding of relevant Animal Ethics, Biosafety, Biosecurity and Workplace Health and Safety requirements, or the ability to rapidly acquire this knowledge About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside some of the brightest minds from around the world in an environment where collaboration, innovation and purpose drive meaningful outcomes. We are committed to creating change together through research excellence, operational excellence and a culture that values diversity of thought and experience. At UQ, you’ll enjoy a workplace where your contribution is recognised and where you can continue to grow your career while making a tangible impact. The broader benefits of joining the UQ community include up to 26 weeks paid parental leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements where operationally appropriate, access to health and wellbeing initiatives, and ongoing professional development opportunities. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Rebecca Bruce at r.bruce1@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks, mandatory immunisations, First Aid and/or CPR certification, and other role-specific checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 26 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-69230)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Security-Officer---Gatton-Campus_R-70033-2": {
      "title": "Security Officer - Gatton Campus",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nProperty and Facilities Division, Security Full-time, continuing and casual leave-coverage roles available Base Salary of $70,318 + 17% super (HEW Level 3) Casual rate of $45.55 per hour, inclusive of 25% casual loading Based at UQ’s Gatton Campus   About UQ's Gatton Campus At The University of Queensland, we are creating change together. UQ Gatton combines the proud rural traditions of its Queensland Agricultural College heritage with innovative teaching and research in agriculture, animal science, veterinary science and the environment. With expansive grounds, specialist facilities and a strong sense of community, Gatton offers a workplace unlike any other UQ campus. You will work alongside passionate, purpose-driven people while helping create a safe, welcoming and supportive environment for students, staff and visitors. About This Opportunity Join the Security team within Property and Facilities and play an important role in protecting the people, facilities and grounds of UQ’s Gatton Campus. We currently have full-time, continuing and casual leave-coverage roles available within our close-knit operational team. This is a varied, hands-on role where no two shifts are quite the same. You may respond to an emergency, assist a student or visitor, patrol campus facilities, investigate an incident or support a major event. Throughout each shift, you will provide a professional, calm and responsive security service that supports the safety and wellbeing of the wider UQ Gatton community. UQ Gatton operates 24 hours a day, seven days a week. We are looking for officers who are comfortable working independently, following established procedures and exercising sound judgement while remaining connected to central security operations. The casual role will provide important coverage during planned leave, unexpected absences and other operational requirements. Please note: Hours for the casual role are not guaranteed, and shifts may be offered at short notice depending on operational requirements. Key responsibilities include: Responding to alarms, medical emergencies, fire emergencies and other incidents as they arise. Locking, patrolling and providing access to authorised personnel across University facilities and grounds. Conducting preliminary investigations and preparing accurate incident reports using established systems and procedures. Providing professional assistance and responding to enquiries from students, staff and members of the public. Enforcing parking and traffic regulations where required. Escalating priority and emergency matters to the Security Supervisor in a timely manner.   Please Note: A successful applicant will need full availability to attend Three weeks of full-time training . About You You are a calm, reliable and approachable security officer who can follow established procedures, work independently when required and make sound decisions when responding to incidents. To be successful in this role, you will demonstrate: A current Queensland Certificate II in Security Operations, including Security Officer Unarmed, Crowd Control, Bodyguard and Monitoring modules Current HLTAID011 Provide First Aid certification Current HLTAID009 Provide Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) certification A current Queensland manual open driver licence The ability to work independently with minimal supervision in a regional campus environment Sound judgement and confident decision-making during emergency and operational situations Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage respectfully with people from diverse backgrounds Experience using security monitoring systems and preparing accurate incident reports Professionalism, resilience, tact and restraint when managing challenging situations Experience responding to emergencies in security, operational or public-facing environments       The successful candidate will be required to complete background checks including right to work in Australia verification and a criminal history check. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this opportunity. What We Can Offer A full-time, continuing position or casual leave-coverage opportunity A supportive, experienced and collaborative security team Comprehensive training and ongoing development Varied work across one of Australia’s most distinctive university campuses The opportunity to make a visible contribution to the safety and wellbeing of the UQ community Employment with one of Australia’s leading universities   Questions? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Mr Andrew Barling at a.barling@pf.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please contact the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number in the subject line. Want to Apply? All applicants must upload the following document for their application to be considered: Resume. Other Information UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges that have impacted, or continue to impact, their career trajectory. Candidates who do not meet every criterion are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. Assessment will consider both performance and potential relative to opportunity. We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We are dedicated to equity, diversity and inclusion, fostering an environment that reflects our wider community. We are committed to attracting, retaining and promoting diverse talent. Please reach out to talent@uq.edu.au if you require accessibility support or adjustments throughout the recruitment process. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-70033)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Coastal-Restoration-Law_R-69621": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Coastal Restoration Law",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nUQ Law School within the Faculty of Business, Economics and Law Part-time (80%), fixed-term position commencing in early January 2027 through to December 2028. Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 to $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A). Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus     About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity to join a visionary, multidisciplinary team working on an Australian Research Council-funded project examining regulatory permitting for marine and coastal restoration. Embedded within a collaborative research environment spanning law, ecology, modelling and social science, you will undertake purposeful legal research that traces existing regulatory requirements, identifies opportunities for reform and helps evaluate how proposed reforms could support effective restoration. You will work closely with academic colleagues, computational modelling experts, government and industry collaborators to create research with practical and globally relevant impact.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Undertake high-quality applied legal research aligned with the aims of the ARC Discovery Project, contribute to the co-design and delivery of the project, and produce research outputs for highly regarded law and environmental policy journals. Research collaboration and engagement: Work collaboratively across law, ecology, modelling and social science, communicate legal research effectively to multidisciplinary audiences, and engage constructively with government, industry and other research end users. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, as appropriate, and support a responsible, inclusive and collaborative research culture. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through active participation in priority activities, internal service and administrative processes, support for colleagues, and the development of partnerships with government, industry, professional bodies and the wider community.   This is a Research Focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in environmental law or a closely related discipline. An emerging research profile in environmental law, marine and coastal law, regulatory law or a related field. Evidence of producing, or the demonstrated potential to produce, high-quality research outputs in reputable refereed journals. Evidence of presenting research at academic conferences, professional forums or other relevant settings. Demonstrated ability to undertake rigorous applied legal research and analyse complex legislation, policy and regulatory frameworks. Experience collaborating across disciplines, ideally with researchers in environmental science, ecology, computational modelling or the social sciences. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to explain legal concepts and findings to multidisciplinary and non-legal audiences. Experience with social science research methodologies, including surveys and interviews, is desirable.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Justine Bell-James at j.bell-james@law.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Monday 9th November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69621). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late November, early December."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Finance-Partner_R-69986-1": {
      "title": "Finance Partner",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nFinance Professional Services Full time (100%); 1 x continuing position, 1 x fixed term position through to October 2027 Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% Superannuation (HEW Level 7)  Based at our St Lucia Campus     About This Opportunity We are seeking a driven and proactive Finance Partner to support the Chief Operating Officer (COO) portfolio. Working within a collaborative and high-performing finance advisory team, you will partner with senior leaders to provide trusted financial insights that support informed decision-making and strategic outcomes. This is an opportunity to build strong partnerships, influence key decisions and contribute to meaningful outcomes across the University. Key responsibilities will include: Providing trusted financial advice to leaders and chief investigators to support cost management and the optimal utilisation of funding. Building and maintaining strong stakeholder relationships, acting as a trusted advisor and managing the escalation of complex financial matters. Applying financial processes, governance frameworks and policy controls to ensure compliance with University and external requirements. Facilitating collaboration between finance advisory and transaction services teams to enhance service delivery, financial performance and strategic outcomes. About You Degree qualifications in accounting, business, commerce or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of extensive training and professional experience. Strong financial accounting, analysis and reporting capability, including experience delivering project-based accounting services. Demonstrated experience within financial advisory, business partnering or service delivery functions operating in complex stakeholder environments. Proven ability to build, influence and maintain productive working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders and clients. Highly developed communication and problem-solving skills, with the ability to present insights clearly and drive informed decision-making. Demonstrated capability to provide strategically aligned financial advice that supports organisational priorities and objectives. Strong understanding of financial governance, compliance requirements, policy frameworks and risk management principles. Progress towards, or membership of, a professional accounting body such as CPA Australia, Chartered Accountants Australia and New Zealand, or equivalent. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dane Cruickshanks at d.cruickshanks@uq.edu.au. For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au, stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section, and whether you are applying for the continuing or fixed-term position. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration, criminal history checks and other relevant checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 26 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-69986)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Laboratory-Assistant--Sample-Management---Lab-Operations-_R-69643": {
      "title": "Laboratory Assistant (Sample Management & Lab Operations)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-12\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Environmental Health Sciences Full-time, fixed-term position through to 31 December 2027   Base salary will range from $79,096 - $83,863 (HEW Level 4) + 17% Superannuation Based at our Dutton Park campus About This Opportunity Are you a practical, organised, safety-conscious and team-focused person? Do you have experience supporting laboratory or research operations? If the answer is yes, we have the ideal role for you! Join the dedicated and supportive research team at the Queensland Alliance for Environmental Health Sciences and help deliver world-class research across environmental toxicology, epidemiology, microbiology, health risk assessment, and state-of-the art monitoring and analytical technologies. As a Laboratory Assistant , you will provide a range of technical, operational and administrative support that keeps our busy laboratories and research programs running safely and efficiently. Working collaboratively with researchers and support staff, you will help manage samples, equipment, consumables and laboratory records, contributing to vital research that identifies and responds to emerging environmental health risks. Key responsibilities will include: Receive, register, organise and store incoming research and environmental samples using laboratory databases and sample management systems. Maintain sampling equipment, laboratory consumables and inventory records, including ordering, receiving and registering new equipment. Prepare and distribute equipment and materials for clients, while maintaining accurate laboratory documents, databases and registers. Support the safe and efficient operation of laboratory facilities, including equipment and facility upkeep, glassware management, and chemical and clinical waste management. Please note, aspects of this role will require work in cold environments, including cold room and -20°C storage areas. About You Completion of a Diploma in Laboratory Technology, or completion of a Certificate IV with relevant work experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education or training. Experience working in a laboratory, research or similar technical environment. Experience using laboratory equipment and supporting equipment maintenance, consumables management, stock control and inventory activities. Experience receiving, recording, handling and storing research or environmental samples. Strong computer and database literacy, including experience maintaining accurate records and using electronic record systems. Excellent attention to detail, with the ability to prioritise and manage multiple tasks accurately. The ability to work independently under routine direction and contribute effectively within a multidisciplinary team. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program and other opportunities for career development and progression.  Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Gabriele Elisei at g.elisei@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (R-69643) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-69643)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Customer-Service-Assistant_R-69823-1": {
      "title": "Customer Service Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nAustralian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology (AIBN) Part-time (60%, W,Th,F required), permanent position with a busy, collaborative team Base salary will be in the range $79,096.42 - $83,863.22 + 17% super (HEW Level 4, pro rata) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  We are seeking an organised and reliable Administration Assistant to work 3 days per week providing essential frontline administrative support for the efficient operation of the Receptions team at the Australian Institute for Bioengineering and Nanotechnology. Working in a busy, collaborative environment, you will provide responsive reception duties, office coordination and administrative support that enables colleagues to focus on research with meaningful impact.   This is a part-time position working Wed, Thurs, Fri per week, with some flexibility for start/finish times and the possibility to work 0.6FTE across 4 days.   Key responsibilities will include:  Provide high-level administrative support to staff under general supervision, using sound judgement to prioritise activities and meet operational needs. Coordinate routine events, meetings and engagement activities, including scheduling, room bookings, documentation, minute-taking, RSVP monitoring and liaison with attendees. Assisting with organising student/visitor travel arrangements when required. Support efficient day-to-day office procedures, logistics and operations in accordance with relevant administrative protocols, quality assurance systems and standards. Liaise with internal and external stakeholders, respond to routine enquiries, welcome and brief visitors, maintain information systems and administer relevant financial transactions in accordance with University policies and procedures.   About You   You will have: A Certificate IV or Diploma with relevant work experience, a Certificate III with extensive relevant work experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate clearly and professionally with diverse stakeholders. Demonstrated experience providing responsive, high-quality customer service and building effective working relationships. Strong organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to prioritise competing tasks and meet deadlines. Well-developed problem-solving skills and a reliable, adaptable approach to day-to-day administrative requirements. A high level of proficiency in Microsoft Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook. Experience coordinating meetings, events, documentation, visitors and office logistics. The ability to follow administrative, financial and quality assurance processes accurately while contributing positively to a supportive and inclusive team.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Lesley Green – l.green@uq.edu.au   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close 18 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69823). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing 26 October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Officer--Corporate-Affairs-and-Stakeholder-Relations_R-69687-1": {
      "title": "Principal Officer, Corporate Affairs and Stakeholder Relations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nMarketing and communication Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $106,709 to $116,199 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Based at our St Lucia campus. About This Opportunity Join UQ’s Corporate Affairs team to deliver strategic communication and stakeholder engagement programs that strengthen and protect the University’s reputation. You’ll contribute across a range of communication and engagement activities, from major infrastructure and campus development projects to corporate initiatives, industry engagement and incident management. You will help to build trusted relationships with key stakeholders including industry, government, partners, staff, students and the wider community while helping communicate UQ’s contribution to Queensland. Key responsibilities will include: Support the development and implementation of communication and stakeholder engagement programs for major infrastructure, campus development and corporate initiatives aligned with UQ’s strategic priorities. Develop clear, purposeful communication plans, materials and assets for a range of audiences, channels and formats. Contribute to industry and government engagement activities that strengthen UQ’s profile, positioning and relationships. Analyse and provide advice on potential stakeholder impacts and support relationship management practices across UQ’s activities. Support reputation management activities and contribute to communication responses during incidents and emerging issues.   About You Tertiary qualifications in communication, public relations, journalism or a related discipline, with at least six years of subsequent relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and industry experience. Proven success in developing and implementing contemporary communication and stakeholder engagement plans for diverse audiences. Experience supporting infrastructure projects that require proactive engagement, careful relationship management and early identification of stakeholder issues. Highly developed written communication skills, with the ability to write clearly and precisely for multiple audiences, channels and purposes. Strong interpersonal and verbal communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to build collaborative and trusted working relationships. Excellent organisational and time-management skills, including the ability to plan, manage a high volume of work and respond effectively to changing priorities. Sound judgement and the ability to analyse potential stakeholder and reputational impacts in complex environments. Knowledge of, or experience in, the Australian higher education sector is desirable but not essential.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a visionary and inclusive community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. UQ’s standing reflects the collective impact of its people and the strength of its relationships. In 2025, UQ was named among the world’s leading universities in the Times Higher Education World Reputation Ranking, rising to fourth in Australia and 78th globally. Read more about UQ’s reputation ranking . Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Rebecca Schrauf at r.schrauf@uq.edu.au   For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69687)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Lecturer-in-Microelectronics_R-69524": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Microelectronics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time permanent position Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 - $141,545.09 + 17% super (Academic Level C) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a visionary and collaborative academic community where you will help create change through world-class teaching, research, and industry engagement in microelectronics. The School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science is seeking an ambitious and inquisitive Senior Lecturer to contribute to the development of future engineers while advancing impactful research in microelectronics and electronic systems.   Working alongside leading researchers, industry partners, and talented students, you will be supported by a connected environment that values innovation, excellence, and meaningful real-world impact. Drawing on UQ’s culture of collaboration and world-leading teaching and research, you will have the opportunity to build a distinctive research profile while contributing to the School’s growing strengths across sensing, instrumentation, communications, healthcare technologies, photonics, computing, and emerging technologies.   Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Establish and grow an internationally recognised research program in microelectronics, publish high-quality research outputs, attract and manage external research funding, contribute to translation and commercialisation activities, and develop strategic collaborations with industry and research partners. Teaching: Develop, coordinate and deliver high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate courses across computer architecture, analogue and mixed-signal design, digital and VLSI design, embedded systems and related areas. Lead curriculum innovation, coordinate courses, provide academic guidance to students, and contribute to continuous improvement of teaching programs. Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise Higher Degree by Research students, foster researcher capability development, demonstrate leadership in responsible research conduct, and provide mentorship and performance support to staff and students. Citizenship and Service: Contribute actively to School, Faculty and University service, build strong external partnerships, mentor colleagues, support staff wellbeing, participate in governance activities, and embody UQ values through leadership and collaboration.   This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  You will bring a strong academic reputation and a demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching, research, supervision and service, together with: A PhD in Microelectronics, Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering, VLSI Design, Electronic Systems Engineering, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in microelectronics and electronic systems, with strengths in areas such as integrated circuit design, VLSI systems, computer architecture, embedded systems, RF systems, sensing technologies, photonics, communications, healthcare technologies, quantum technologies, or related fields. Expertise in analogue and mixed-signal integrated circuit design, digital and VLSI design, embedded systems, system-on-chip architectures, or related technologies, with experience using industry-standard electronic design automation tools considered advantageous. An established record of high-quality research outputs and growing international recognition through publication in leading journals and conferences. Demonstrated success in securing, managing, or contributing to competitive research funding opportunities. Evidence of high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, including curriculum design, course coordination, and innovative approaches to learning and assessment. A successful track record of supervising Honours and Higher Degree by Research students to completion. Evidence of leadership, collaboration, and meaningful contributions to internal service, external engagement, industry partnerships, and professional activities.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Markus Barth m.barth@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume including your public Google Scholar profile , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Monday 9 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69524). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for late November to early December 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Languages-and-Cultures_R-69874": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Languages and Cultures",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-09\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nSchool of Languages and Cultures Casual Academic positions About This Opportunity The School of Languages and Cultures (SLC) is seeking Casual academics across the discipline below: Undergraduate Language, Technology and Global Communication - specifically recruiting for the course WRIT1001 – Writing for International Students   Casual academics provide a rich and important contribution to the University’s academic, teaching, research, artistic, outreach and clinical endeavours through the provision of a range of academically focused services and activities. In SLC, Casual academics may be engaged to fulfil a wide range of duties, including (but not limited to) tutoring; marking; demonstrating; teaching; student outreach and engagement; moderating/facilitating and/or student advising across multiple academic disciplines. Hours will vary according to the class timetable. SLC classes typically run between 8:00 am and 4:50pm with occasional evening classes beginning at 5:00pm or later for the entire semester. A commitment for the whole of the semester is required. Please click here for details of the programs and courses offered in the School of Languages and Cultures. Please note that these opportunities are subject to The University’s Code of Conduct . About You You must possess a minimum of an undergraduate degree, preferably in an area relevant to the discipline in which you seek to deliver content. You must have demonstrated language ability commensurate with the requirements of a given course and have relatively recent experience with the course material or a demonstrated capacity to become familiar with the material in a short time. You might be studying at Honours or postgraduate level at UQ or another university or be working as a research assistant or postdoctoral fellow. Perhaps you have taught in your field at a significant level in the past and are looking to return to the workforce. The selection process involves assessment of your application where consideration will be given to the following factors, in no particular order: Current postgraduate students whose supervisors approve the time spent teaching; Outstanding student feedback from prior courses; Demonstrated flexibility and ability to adapt to different teaching modes; Formal training and practical experience in teaching; Demonstrated expertise or relevant education in the course subject; For new postgraduate students: lack of other tutoring or academic work experience. What We Can Offer These are casual appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. Casual academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement and reiterated in this job profile. Please click here to view our various casual academic pay scales. The current pay rate is $57.60 per hour ($68.13 for PhD-qualified). Some training is provided. For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 (Enterprise Agreement) Questions? For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au  stating the job reference number in the subject line. Other Information You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. At UQ we know that our greatest strengths come from our diverse mix of colleagues, this is reflected in our ongoing commitment to creating an environment focused on equity, diversity and inclusion .  We ensure that we are always attracting, retaining and promoting colleagues who are representative of the diversity in our broader community, whether that be  gender identity ,  LGBTQIA+ ,  cultural and/or linguistic ,  Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander peoples , or  people with a disability . Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au . How to Apply Applications should be made using the apply button below. If you are a current employee of the University, or hold an unpaid or affiliate appointment with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board. You must include an updated CV, supporting statements or other evidence such as previous recent teaching evaluations as required.  Please also indicate which course(s) you may be interested in teaching. All applications will be considered. Those accepted will be included in the School of Languages and Cultures Talent Pool for the year 2027 unless you indicate that you do not wish for your application to be held on file. Please note if you are interested in being included in the SLC Talent Pool for future years you will need to reapply each year. Please note, an application does not guarantee a position. If your application is successful, you will then be contacted by relevant Program and Major Convenors with details of specific course activities that are available within the given Program or Major/s in which you have expressed interest. You will be requested to identify for which activities you wish to be considered and to indicate your availability to undertake them in the relevant Semester. You will be advised of the relevant due date for this process. Applications Close on 11 December 2026 at 11:59PM AEST"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Gatton-Campus/Trainer-and-Assessor---BSB50420-Diploma-of-Leadership-and-Management---Casual_R-68639": {
      "title": "Trainer and Assessor – BSB50420 Diploma of Leadership and Management - Casual",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nUQ Skills . Casual position. This role supports the delivery of training and assessment in a remote environment.   About This Opportunity. Join UQ Skills as a casual Trainer and Assessor, specialising in the delivery and assessment of the BSB50420 Diploma of Leadership and Management. Working within a collaborative vocational education environment, you will help learners develop practical leadership capabilities through purposeful, engaging and industry-relevant training delivered on campus, online, remotely and at off-campus locations.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Deliver engaging training using approved training plans, lesson plans, learning resources, equipment and practical demonstrations. Assess and mark learner work, provide constructive feedback, manage reassessment requirements, and monitor attendance, progress and results in consultation with the Training Manager. Contribute expert advice to the development, validation and moderation of training and assessment processes, tools and resources to support quality and continuous improvement. Complete required administration through aXcelerate, maintain the confidentiality of learner and assessment materials, and comply with UQ Skills policies, procedures and Australian Skills Quality Authority requirements.   About You. Industry qualifications within your area of expertise at least one level higher than the qualification being delivered and assessed, or an equivalent combination of extensive relevant industry experience and qualifications. A Certificate IV in Training and Assessment TAE40116, or TAE40110 with TAELLN411 and TAEASS502, or a higher qualification in adult and vocational education. Recent and relevant industry and vocational education and training experience. Knowledge of Australian Skills Quality Authority standards relating to learning, assessment and the mapping of training materials, with the ability to apply these requirements in practice. Demonstrated knowledge of the Principles of Assessment and Rules of Evidence, or the ability to rapidly acquire and apply this knowledge. Experience using learning management systems and student management systems, together with strong capability across Microsoft Office applications. High-level written, verbal, organisational and interpersonal skills, including the ability to build cooperative working relationships, manage competing priorities and maintain close attention to detail. The ability to engage, influence and motivate learners and present contemporary teaching material in a meaningful, accessible and stimulating way.   About UQ. As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world.   Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as access to exclusive internal-only vacancies,   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Eimear Vallely at eimear.vallely@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information. As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.   Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 8 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68639).   Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Animal-Technical-Assistant_R-69637-1": {
      "title": "Animal Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nBiological Resources Full-time, fixed-term position up to 25/06/2027 Base salary will be in the range $79,096 - $83,863 + 17% super (HEW Level 4). Based at our Dutton Park location. About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team supporting world-class biomedical and scientific research through the delivery of exceptional animal care and technical services. As an Animal Technical Assistant, you will contribute to a regulated research environment where animal welfare, operational excellence, compliance and teamwork are at the heart of everything we do. Working alongside researchers, supervisors and fellow technical professionals, you will help create the conditions that enable impactful scientific discoveries while upholding the highest standards of care and biosecurity. Key responsibilities will include : Deliver high-quality animal care, husbandry and breeding services for laboratory rodents and other species, ensuring optimal animal welfare, colony health and accurate record-keeping. Perform routine and technical procedures, including injections, sample collection, breeding support and euthanasia, in accordance with approved protocols and regulatory requirements. Maintain facility operations, cleanliness and biosecurity by operating equipment, monitoring life-support systems, managing inventory and responding promptly to alarms or incidents. Provide effective operational and compliance support to researchers and stakeholders through clear communication, sound technical advice and collaborative teamwork, including participation in rostered after-hours work. About You Relevant qualifications in animal technology, biological sciences or a related discipline, and/or an equivalent combination of training and practical experience. Demonstrated capability to work confidently within a regulated laboratory animal environment while maintaining high standards of animal welfare. Sound knowledge of animal breeding programs, colony management and record-keeping processes, or the ability to rapidly develop this expertise. Experience handling laboratory animals and performing routine technical procedures with accuracy, care and professionalism. Strong understanding of biosecurity, workplace health and safety, and compliance requirements relevant to animal research environments. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build collaborative working relationships with researchers, technical staff and stakeholders. Strong organisational skills with the ability to prioritise competing tasks, maintain attention to detail and work both independently and as part of a team. A commitment to service excellence, continuous improvement and supporting research outcomes that create meaningful impact. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, Rebecca Bruce, r.bruce1@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Working with laboratory animals is an inherent requirement of the position. The appointment is therefore subject to, and conditional upon, satisfactory medical clearance(s), including disclosure of relevant medical history, to undertake such work without unreasonable risk to your health and safety. In addition, the Employee is required to fully comply with necessary ongoing health monitoring procedures and control measures associated with the position. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Thursday 22 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69637)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Administration-Officer_R-68336-2": {
      "title": "Administration Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAbout This Opportunity    The Administration Officer provides high-quality administrative, stakeholder engagement and event support to the Medical School and its Mayne Academies. Working within a collaborative, agile and inclusive team, you will help deliver operational excellence across School activities, governance processes, strategic initiatives and community engagement programs. In line with UQ’s commitment to creating change together, this role offers the opportunity to contribute to a connected, supportive and purpose-driven environment that values collaboration, responsiveness and continuous improvement Key responsibilities will include :  Providing high-level administrative support across School initiatives, projects, operational activities and events. Coordinating committee and governance support, including meeting administration, documentation, minutes and action tracking. Supporting the Deputy Head of School (Clinical Engagement) and Mayne Academy Heads through travel, event and financial administration. Maintaining effective stakeholder communications, website and intranet content, and assisting with engagement and recognition initiatives. This is a full-time, fixed-term position until December 2028 at HEW Level 5.  About You As the Administration Officer, you will be a highly motivated and collaborative professional who thrives in a dynamic environment and is committed to delivering exceptional administrative and stakeholder support. You will bring: An undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training. Demonstrated experience providing high-quality administrative and organisational support in a complex, service-focused environment. Excellent interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. Strong organisational, time management and problem-solving skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands and meet deadlines with accuracy and attention to detail. Proficiency in the Microsoft Office suite, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint and Outlook, together with the ability to quickly learn new systems, databases and processes. Experience interpreting policies and procedures, exercising sound judgement, and working both independently and collaboratively to deliver positive outcomes.  The successful candidate may be required to complete a number of pre-employment checks, including right to work in Australia and a criminal check. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.    Please note that this role will require occasional out-of-hours work to support events and related activities. Flexibility to work outside standard business hours on occasion is essential. Questions?    For more information about this opportunity, please contact Kendall Hepple at k.hepple@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job requisition ID in the subject line.     Want to Apply?   We welcome applications from all individuals and are committed to an inclusive and accessible recruitment process. To be considered, please ensure you upload:       Resume  A cover letter summarising how your background aligns with the 'About You' section      Our strength as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. If you require an alternative method to submit your application due to accessibility needs or personal circumstances, please contact talent@uq.edu.au .      Other Information    UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.    Applications close Saturday 17 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68336). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week of 19th October 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Remote-University-Sites---Off-Campus/Casual-Trainer---Assessor--Procurement-and-Contracting-_R-68327-1": {
      "title": "Casual Trainer & Assessor (Procurement and Contracting)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nUQ Skills Casual position. This role supports the delivery of training in a remote environment.   About This Opportunity  UQ Skills is seeking a collaborative, purposeful and industry-connected Trainer and Assessor to support the delivery and assessment of nationally recognised procurement and contracting qualifications. Working within a supportive and inclusive Registered Training Organisation, you will play a key role in developing capability across government and industry learners while contributing to UQ’s vision of creating meaningful change through high-quality vocational education. Drawing on your expertise, you will help learners build practical skills and confidence while supporting continuous improvement across training and assessment practices.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to:  Deliver and/or assess PSP40616 Certificate IV in Procurement and Contracting, PSP50616 Diploma of Procurement and Contracting and PSP60616 Advanced Diploma of Procurement and Contracting. Prepare and maintain training plans, lesson plans, learning resources, assessment tools and training materials. Monitor student attendance, progress and assessment outcomes, providing constructive feedback and managing reassessment activities where required. Participate in validation, moderation and continuous improvement activities to ensure compliance and quality outcomes. Complete student administration and record management activities, including use of aXcelerate and associated systems.   About You Industry qualifications in procurement and contracting at least one level higher than the qualification you train and assess in, or equivalent extensive industry experience and qualifications. Certificate IV in Training and Assessment TAE40116, or TAE40110 with the required upgrades including TAELLN411 and TAEASS502, or higher qualifications in adult and vocational education. Recent and relevant industry and vocational education and training experience. Knowledge of ASQA standards and demonstrated ability to apply quality training, assessment and mapping practices. Understanding of the Principles of Assessment and Rules of Evidence. Experience using Learning Management Systems and Student Management Systems. Strong written and verbal communication skills, organisational capability and attention to detail. Ability to motivate learners and present engaging, practical and meaningful training content.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world.   Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as access to exclusive internal-only vacancies and affordable parking from just $6.40 a day.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Eimear Vallely at Eimear.vallely@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information   As a casual employee, and in line with Fair Work , please note that there is no commitment to ongoing work in this position.    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 8 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68327).   Please note applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Dutton-Park/Lecturers-in-Pharmacy-Practice_R-69865-2": {
      "title": "Lecturers in Pharmacy Practice",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSchool of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences Two full-time, fixed-term positions for up to three years commencing January 2027 Base salary will range from $119,462 - $141,545 (Academic Level B) + 17% Superannuation Based at our Dutton Park campus About This Opportunity Our School of Pharmacy and Pharmaceutical Sciences is seeking two experienced and inspiring Lecturers in Pharmacy Practice to support the development of UQ’s new Doctor of Pharmacy program. Working within a collaborative and inclusive academic team, you will shape contemporary pharmacy education, mentor the next generation of pharmacy professionals and pursue impactful research that advances clinical practice and community outcomes. Key responsibilities will include: Teaching: Develop and deliver engaging courses in pharmacy practice and cognate disciplines, including clinical therapeutics, professional practice, the social and ethical dimensions of practice and clinical epidemiology. Coordinate courses, assess students and provide high-quality academic advice and support. Research: Build an independent or collaborative research program with national recognition and impact. Contribute to competitive funding applications, publish in reputable journals and use best-practice research methodologies. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, supporting their capabilities, professional growth and responsible conduct of research. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships with colleagues, industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community. Contribute to School service, mentor others and consistently demonstrate UQ’s values. This is a teaching & research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You A PhD in pharmacy, pharmacy practice or a related field. Experience and evidence of high-quality teaching at undergraduate or postgraduate level. Professional experience and expertise enabling you to teach pharmacy practice and cognate disciplines, including but not limited to, clinical therapeutics, professional practice, social and ethical dimensions of practice and clinical epidemiology. Demonstrated experience in course coordination, contemporary curriculum design and the development or continuous improvement of pharmacy programs and course materials. A developing reputation and track record of publications in reputable refereed journals and presentations at conferences. Evidence of successfully seeking, obtaining and managing external research funding. A growing record of supervising Research Higher Degree students. Experience leading internal service roles at a School level and contributing to external activities within the wider profession. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   At UQ, you will join a visionary and collaborative community committed to creating change together. You will be supported to pursue innovative teaching, meaningful research and professional growth while helping prepare future pharmacy professionals to make a positive impact.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Peter Cabot, Head of School, at pcabot@pharmacy.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number R-69865.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter including responses to the ‘About You’ Selection Criteria above . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, your qualifications and full AHPRA registration. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Friday 30 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number R-69865)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/The-Park-Centre-for-Mental-Health---Wacol/Senior-Research-Fellow---Evaluation-Research-Stream_R-69530-1": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow - Evaluation Research Stream",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nQueensland Centre for Mental Health Research within the School of Public Health . Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for 12 months. Base salary will be in the range $145,962 to $168,044 + 17% super (Academic Level C). Based at our Wacol location About This Opportunity This is an exciting opportunity for a Senior Research Fellow to join the Evaluation Research Stream at the Queensland Centre for Mental Health Research. You will bring specialist quantitative research expertise to a collaborative and purposeful program of applied research and evaluation focused on mental health service improvement and system reform. A key initial focus will be the Health Translation Queensland-funded Mental Health and Emergency Department project, where your work will help illuminate how different mental health service ecosystems influence emergency department patient flow and access, identify gaps and populations not adequately served by current arrangements, and shape evidence-informed options for new or enhanced models of care. This role is ideally suited to someone with strong project management, initiative and stakeholder engagement skills, and the ability to work independently while collaborating effectively with researchers, clinicians, data custodians, health services and other partners. Key responsibilities will include: Research: Lead the quantitative components of the project, including developing research questions, analytic frameworks, outcome measures and analysis plans, and applying contemporary quantitative and health services research methods to routinely collected Queensland Health data. Research translation and collaboration: Integrate quantitative findings with other project evidence, work closely with researchers, health services, clinicians, data custodians and partners, and translate rigorous analysis into meaningful findings for mental health policy and service improvement. Supervision and Researcher Development: Provide quantitative methodological leadership, advice and mentorship to research staff, students and collaborators, while promoting responsible research conduct, reproducibility, research integrity and sound data governance. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships, demonstrate leadership through mentoring and collaboration, contribute to internal service and decision-making, support colleagues, and consistently uphold UQ’s values. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance. About You Completion of a PhD in epidemiology, biostatistics, health services research, public health, mental health, health sciences or another relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of relevant qualifications and substantial research experience. An established profile in quantitative health services, epidemiological, evaluation or related applied health research. Demonstrated expertise in analysing and interpreting large and complex routinely collected health or administrative datasets, including the development of research questions, analysis plans, variables, indicators and outcome measures. Strong knowledge of quantitative research design and statistical methods, with the ability to select, apply and interpret approaches appropriate to complex health services research questions. Demonstrated ability to critically assess data quality, completeness, bias, confounding and other limitations, and appropriately account for these in analysis, interpretation and communication of findings. Experience with data governance, ethics, privacy and data access requirements associated with routinely collected or administrative health data. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Zoe Rutherford . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information. Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69530). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for Thursday 5th November 2026."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow---Research-Fellow-in-Agricultural-Systems-Assessment_R-68711-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Fellow in Agricultural Systems Assessment",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation. Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 plus 17% super at Academic Level A, or $119,463 to $141,545 plus 17% super at Academic Level B. Based at our St Lucia location .   About This Opportunity. Join a purposeful and collaborative research environment within QAAFI’s Centre for Crop Science and contribute to SorGain, a research, development and extension program co-funded by GRDC and UQ to improve Australian sorghum production. You will lead the day-to-day delivery and integration of the “Knowledge Integration, Grower Insight and Decision Frameworks” work package, drawing together crop agronomy, adaptive research, stakeholder decision research and advanced analytics to create practical, regionally relevant outcomes for growers and industry.   Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Research: Conduct regional evidence syntheses and meta-analyses of sorghum agronomy, integrate agronomic, survey and decision data using qualitative, mixed-method, statistical and machine-learning approaches, and translate findings into transparent and practical decision-support resources. Research: Lead stakeholder decision dynamics research with growers, advisers and industry representatives, including research design, sampling, ethics, quality assurance, interviews, focus groups and participatory workshops. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, research assistants and project staff through methodological advice, mentoring, constructive feedback and shared planning. Citizenship and Service: Coordinate project activities, milestones, risks and reporting, contribute to publications and funding proposals, represent SorGain and QAAFI at scientific and industry forums, and actively support the priorities of the Centre for Crop Science.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   For further information, please click here to view the information booklet .   About You. A PhD, or a doctoral thesis submitted, in agronomy, crop science, farming systems, agricultural systems science, agricultural extension or innovation, decision science, or another closely related discipline with a substantial applied crop-agronomy component. Demonstrated expertise in crop agronomy and broadacre farming systems, including the ability to interpret interactions among crop genotype, environment, management, operational constraints and seasonal risk. Experience in adaptive, participatory or on-farm research, with the ability to translate agronomic evidence and stakeholder knowledge into well-defined research questions, practical study designs and iterative learning activities. Experience designing and analysing qualitative or mixed-method research involving growers, advisers or other agricultural stakeholders, including interviews, focus groups, workshops or surveys. Strong quantitative and computational skills, including demonstrated ability to analyse complex agronomic, environmental, survey or behavioural data using R, Python or equivalent software. Experience in evidence synthesis, meta-analysis or the integration of heterogeneous research and field datasets, with careful attention to study design, data quality, uncertainty and reproducibility. Demonstrated ability to engage respectfully and productively with growers, advisers, industry, government and research partners, together with the capacity to undertake regular travel to sorghum-growing regions of Queensland and New South Wales. An emerging or established record of high-quality peer-reviewed publications, with the ability to prepare clear technical reports, presentations and stakeholder-focused materials.   For appointment at Academic Level B, you will also demonstrate: At least 3 years of independent and collaborative research beyond the doctoral project, supported by peer-reviewed publications or other substantial scholarly outputs. Experience leading significant research components, coordinating multidisciplinary contributors and external stakeholders, and taking responsibility for the quality and timely delivery of outputs. Evidence of contributions to postgraduate supervision or research training, funding proposals, partnership development or other forms of external research support.   Knowledge of sorghum or other summer cereal production in dryland broadacre systems would be advantageous, as would experience in field trials, on-farm experimentation, farmer-participatory research, survey methodologies, research ethics, monitoring and evaluation, relational databases, SQL or AI-supported decision-support systems.   About UQ. As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   At QAAFI, we work in partnership to translate visionary crop research into outcomes that respond to grower needs and strengthen Australian agriculture. One such partnership is the Sorghum Strategic Alliance, a seven-year collaboration between UQ and the Grains Research and Development Corporation (GRDC) that brings together expertise in genetics, breeding and agronomy to improve sorghum productivity, resilience and grain quality. Read more about the Sorghum Strategic Alliance   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad, from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Sudhir Yadav sudhir.yadav@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section . Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information. Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Sunday 25 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68711)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Officer---Grain-Refinement-of-Ferrous-Alloys_R-69773": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer - Grain Refinement of Ferrous Alloys",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nSchool of Mechanical and Mining Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Part-time (50%), fixed-term position for up to 12 months Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity for a part-time (0.5 FTE) Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Officer to join a collaborative ARC Linkage project developing innovative grain refiners for ferrous alloys.  You will be working alongside leading researchers and industry partners, conduct experimental metallurgical research, perform microstructural analysis, and help deliver solutions to real-world manufacturing challenges. Advance your research career by working on industry-driven innovation, publishing high-quality research, and developing valuable academic and industry connections.   Key responsibilities will include:    Research: Contribute to an ARC Linkage project developing new grain refiners for ferrous alloys through experimental research, laboratory testing and analysis. Working with university and industry partners, you will deliver project outcomes, report findings, and communicate results through publications and presentations.   Teaching: Participate in undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, maintaining high teaching quality and responding to student feedback.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and HDR students, providing mentoring, feedback and professional support, while effectively managing research staff and resolving workplace issues in line with University policies and procedures.   Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through collaboration, service and active participation in unit activities, while supporting colleagues, contributing to committees and administrative processes, and building relationships with industry, government and community partners.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  Completion or near completion of a PhD in materials or mechanical engineering or related areas. An emerging profile in research in materials engineering. Evidence of publications in reputed refereed journals and presenting at conferences. Evidence of contributions towards successfully obtaining external research funding. Some experience in meaningful internal service roles and contributions towards external activities.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Yahia Ali via y.ali@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications, professional registration checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday, 21 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69773)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Clinical-Educators---Speech-Pathology---Telerehabilitation_R-68520-1": {
      "title": "Clinical Educators - Speech Pathology & Telerehabilitation",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nSchool of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences | UQ Health and Rehabilitation Clinics Two part-year, part-time (40% FTE), continuing positions at HEW Level 7 Base salary will range from $106,709 - $116,198 (full-time equivalent) + 17% Superannuation Primarily based at our vibrant St Lucia campus with some work at external sites as required   About This Opportunity    The School of Health and Rehabilitation Sciences welcomes applications for Clinical Educators in Speech Pathology to support the practice education and clinical development of our speech pathology students on a part-year, continuing basis. We are specifically looking for Speech Pathologists who can deliver: Position 1: Fluency services across the lifespan and general paediatric speech pathology services in the UQ Clinics delivered via telehealth Position 2: Paediatric speech pathology services to paediatric and school-aged children either in local schools or in the UQ Clinics Key responsibilities will include:    Practice Education & Student Supervision: Facilitate and supervise speech pathology and interprofessional clinical placements; monitor, assess and support student learning, skill development and clinical performance; and deliver clinical supervision, professional development activities and work integrated learning support.   Clinical Practice & Service Delivery: Deliver evidence-based speech pathology services in-person and/or via telehealth, support continuity of care, and provide clinical leadership, guidance and oversight of operational and clinical procedures.   Research & Industry Engagement: Contribute to research in clinical practice or practice education, and foster relationships with industry partners, professional bodies, government and the wider community.   Administration & Governance: Contribute to clinic and school operations, manage student placement and assessment administration, and maintain compliance with professional, legislative and organisational standards. Please note, Speech Pathology student-led clinics operate on specific days of the week due to student timetabling. Successful applicants must be available to work on a Thursday commencing January 2027. As these are part-year appointments, it is expected that planned leave will be taken outside of the primary clinical teaching periods that align with the academic calendar or clinical placement periods. Successful applicants will be required to take a combination of paid and unpaid leave over the November to January period, and during the mid-year break. Please take this into consideration before submitting your application. About You Qualifications and training equivalent to an undergraduate/graduate-entry degree in Speech Pathology with at least four (4) years subsequent relevant experience. Demonstrated recency and currency of practice with advanced knowledge and ability to deliver evidence based clinical practice across a range of clinical areas, including telerehabilitation. Demonstrated abilities as an effective educator and communicator in the area of speech pathology practice education such as student supervision, educational evaluation, training and assessment marking. Demonstrated knowledge of clinical education issues and blended learning approaches, with the ability to develop and implement innovative clinical teaching methods that enhance students' clinical skills and knowledge development. Ability to establish effective relationships and to represent and promote the clinical discipline at an interprofessional, university and wider community level, including industry, government and professional bodies. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and autonomously, effectively managing competing priorities and multiple tasks while utilising technology systems and software applications to achieve outcomes.   Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Andrea Abel, Clinic Manager (Speech Pathology & Telerehabilitation), at andrea.whitehead@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (R-68520).    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter. Your cover letter should indicate the position/s you are applying for (Position 1 and/or 2) along with responses to the ‘About You’ selection criteria above. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information   Pre‑employment checks will include verification of your right to work in Australia, qualifications, Speech Pathology Australia membership and current CPSP status, a Working with Children Blue Card and Drivers Licence, eligibility for a Medicare Provider Number and completion of mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender‑based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling 07 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 21 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (Job Reference Number: R-68520)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Casual-Academics---School-of-Political-Science-and-International-Studies_R-69983": {
      "title": "Casual Academics - School of Political Science and International Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-07\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nCasual\n\nSchool of Political Science & International Studies Multiple Casual Academic positions Closing date 8 January 2027 at 11:59pm AEST About This Opportunity    This is a call for applicants for Casual academics in the School of Political Science and International Studies in support of our course offerings in Semester 1 2027.   Casual academics will typically be engaged in a range of duties, including (but not limited to) tutoring; marking; teaching and moderating/facilitating. Casual academics are remunerated according to the category of their duties, as detailed in UQ’s Enterprise Agreement.   Please click here for a list of POLS courses, plus one PPES course which are likely to need tutors. Please indicate a maximum of six courses you are interested in teaching into. Although you will be asked to state why you have picked a course your response is optional.   NB: Tutoring opportunities are reliant on course enrolment numbers and some courses at postgraduate level may not attract sufficient enrolments to require a tutor.   Please note that these opportunities are subject to The University’s Code of Conduct .    What We Can Offer    These are casual appointments. As such, there is no firm advance commitment to ongoing or indefinite work. The casual rate will vary as per the work completed. Please click here to view our various casual academic pay scales. For further information about UQ’s benefits, please visit Why Work at UQ and review  The University of Queensland Enterprise Agreement 2021-2026 . Questions?    For application queries, please contact casualrecruit@uq.edu.au stating the job reference number in the subject line.   Other Information    You must have work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    We value diversity and inclusion, and actively encourage applications from those who bring diversity to the University. Our Diversity and Inclusion webpage contains further information if you require additional support. Accessibility requirements and/or adjustments can be directed to recruitment@uq.edu.au .   Applicants please note:   New applicants use the Apply button  Existing casual staff login via your staff Workday account and apply via the internal careers board.     If you are a current employee of the University (including casual staff), or hold a HDR Scholarship with the University, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Stem-Cell-Population-Genetics_R-69544": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Stem Cell Population Genetics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-06\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nInstitute for Molecular Bioscience Full-time fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus   About This Opportunity  Join a visionary, collaborative and rapidly growing research team led by Dr Drew Neavin at the intersection of population genetics, stem cell biology and single-cell genomics. The Context-dependent Genetics Lab is pioneering innovative “village-in-a-dish” stem cell culture systems that enable researchers to study how genetic regulation changes across cellular contexts, disease states and human genetic diversity. Supported by world-class infrastructure, a connected network of like-minded researchers, and a culture of collaboration and excellence, this role offers an exceptional opportunity to contribute to impactful science while shaping your own research career.   Key responsibilities will include: Research: Design, lead and troubleshoot large-scale stem cell experiments, including population-scale iPSC culture, differentiation and single-cell profiling; contribute to project direction across disease systems; publish high-quality research outputs; analyse single-cell datasets in collaboration with computational researchers; develop robust and reproducible protocols; and contribute to research translation and commercialisation activities. Supervision and Researcher Development: Mentor junior researchers and students in stem cell culture and experimental design; contribute to the supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students as appropriate. Citizenship and Service: Foster a safe, inclusive and collegial laboratory environment; contribute to shared infrastructure and laboratory practices; demonstrate behaviours aligned with UQ values; and develop partnerships with industry, government and the broader research community.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in Stem Cell Biology, Genetics, Genomics, Cell Biology, Computational Biology or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated hands-on experience with human pluripotent stem cell culture and differentiation. Experience planning, executing and troubleshooting large-scale or technically complex biological experiments. Evidence of high-quality publications and conference presentations consistent with career stage. Demonstrated ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary and highly collaborative research team. Experience mentoring, supervising or supporting the development of students or junior researchers. Excellent communication, organisation, collaboration and problem-solving skills. An emerging research profile in stem cells, genomics, genetics, or closely related fields. Highly regarded Experience with single-cell technologies, including single-cell RNA sequencing or multiome approaches. Bioinformatic or computational experience in for example R or Python, or analysis of single-cell or genomic datasets or a willingness to develop these skills with support from the team. Experience with organoid or other complex differentiation systems.  About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Drew Neavin at d.neavin@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Wednesday 4 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69544). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for mid to late November."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-Research-Officer---Hybrid-Glass-X-ray-Scintillators_R-69233-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow/Research Officer - Hybrid Glass X-ray Scintillators",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-06\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSchool of Chemical Engineering – Faculty of EAIT Full-time (100%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Visa sponsorship may be offered for this position Based at our St Lucia Campus - Brisbane   About This Opportunity  The School of Chemical Engineering is seeking a Postdoctoral Research Fellow / Research Officer to join Associate Professor Jingwei Hou's group on the NHMRC Investigator Grant \"Wearable and High-Sensitivity X-ray Detectors for Managing Respiratory Diseases in Infants and Children\".   The project develops hybrid glass scintillators that can be processed into flexible, wearable detectors for low-dose X-ray imaging of children. You will lead the materials work: designing, synthesising and characterising hybrid glass scintillators, and relating glass structure to scintillation performance.   Key responsibilities will include:    Research: Develop and characterise advanced hybrid glass scintillator materials, leading synchrotron experiments, collaborating on detector prototype development, and contributing to high-impact publications, conference presentations, and research funding applications.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise HDR and undergraduate students, supporting researcher development and fostering high performance through mentoring, feedback and professional development. You will also contribute to the effective management of research support staff and help resolve workplace issues in line with University policies and procedures.   Citizenship and Service: Contribute to a positive and collaborative research environment by supporting unit operations, maintaining compliance with health and safety requirements and engaging with internal and external stakeholders to advance the University's goals.   Teaching: Contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, delivering high-quality learning experiences and continuously improving teaching practice in response to student feedback and outcomes.   This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance .   About You  A PhD (awarded or near completion) in materials science, chemistry, chemical engineering or a related field. In-depth understanding of hybrid glass materials, such as MOF glasses, coordination polymer glasses or hybrid metal halide glasses, including melt-quenching and structure-property analysis. Hands-on experience with synchrotron techniques, from beamtime proposals through data collection to analysis. A publication record in relevant journals appropriate to career stage. Ability to work independently and in a multidisciplinary team. Experience with MOF glass synthesis and characterisation, thin-film or flexible device fabrication, and structural modelling of amorphous materials, such as reverse Monte Carlo or molecular dynamics would be considered favourably.   We particularly welcome applications from women, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, members of the LGBTQIA+ community, and others who may be underrepresented in this discipline.   About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Jingwei Hou via jingwei.hou@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.    When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.   Other Information  Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualifications checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here .   We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Thursday, 5 November 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-69233)."
    }
  },
